        -:24727:** This file contains the C functions that implement date and time
        -:24728:** functions for SQLite.
        -:24729:**
        -:24730:** There is only one exported symbol in this file - the function
        -:24731:** sqlite3RegisterDateTimeFunctions() found at the bottom of the file.
        -:24732:** All other code has file scope.
        -:24733:**
        -:24734:** SQLite processes all times and dates as julian day numbers.  The
        -:24735:** dates and times are stored as the number of days since noon
        -:24736:** in Greenwich on November 24, 4714 B.C. according to the Gregorian
        -:24737:** calendar system.
        -:24738:**
        -:24739:** 1970-01-01 00:00:00 is JD 2440587.5
        -:24740:** 2000-01-01 00:00:00 is JD 2451544.5
        -:24741:**
        -:24742:** This implementation requires years to be expressed as a 4-digit number
        -:24743:** which means that only dates between 0000-01-01 and 9999-12-31 can
        -:24744:** be represented, even though julian day numbers allow a much wider
        -:24745:** range of dates.
        -:24746:**
        -:24747:** The Gregorian calendar system is used for all dates and times,
        -:24748:** even those that predate the Gregorian calendar.  Historians usually
        -:24749:** use the julian calendar for dates prior to 1582-10-15 and for some
        -:24750:** dates afterwards, depending on locale.  Beware of this difference.
        -:24751:**
        -:24752:** The conversion algorithms are implemented based on descriptions
        -:24753:** in the following text:
        -:24754:**
        -:24755:**      Jean Meeus
        -:24756:**      Astronomical Algorithms, 2nd Edition, 1998
        -:24757:**      ISBN 0-943396-61-1
        -:24758:**      Willmann-Bell, Inc
        -:24759:**      Richmond, Virginia (USA)
        -:24760:*/
