        -:39914:******************************************************************************/
        -:39915:
        -:39916:
        -:39917:/******************************************************************************
        -:39918:*************************** Posix Advisory Locking ****************************
        -:39919:**
        -:39920:** POSIX advisory locks are broken by design.  ANSI STD 1003.1 (1996)
        -:39921:** section 6.5.2.2 lines 483 through 490 specify that when a process
        -:39922:** sets or clears a lock, that operation overrides any prior locks set
        -:39923:** by the same process.  It does not explicitly say so, but this implies
        -:39924:** that it overrides locks set by the same process using a different
        -:39925:** file descriptor.  Consider this test case:
        -:39926:**
        -:39927:**       int fd1 = open("./file1", O_RDWR|O_CREAT, 0644);
        -:39928:**       int fd2 = open("./file2", O_RDWR|O_CREAT, 0644);
        -:39929:**
        -:39930:** Suppose ./file1 and ./file2 are really the same file (because
        -:39931:** one is a hard or symbolic link to the other) then if you set
        -:39932:** an exclusive lock on fd1, then try to get an exclusive lock
        -:39933:** on fd2, it works.  I would have expected the second lock to
        -:39934:** fail since there was already a lock on the file due to fd1.
        -:39935:** But not so.  Since both locks came from the same process, the
        -:39936:** second overrides the first, even though they were on different
        -:39937:** file descriptors opened on different file names.
        -:39938:**
        -:39939:** This means that we cannot use POSIX locks to synchronize file access
        -:39940:** among competing threads of the same process.  POSIX locks will work fine
        -:39941:** to synchronize access for threads in separate processes, but not
        -:39942:** threads within the same process.
        -:39943:**
        -:39944:** To work around the problem, SQLite has to manage file locks internally
        -:39945:** on its own.  Whenever a new database is opened, we have to find the
        -:39946:** specific inode of the database file (the inode is determined by the
        -:39947:** st_dev and st_ino fields of the stat structure that fstat() fills in)
        -:39948:** and check for locks already existing on that inode.  When locks are
        -:39949:** created or removed, we have to look at our own internal record of the
        -:39950:** locks to see if another thread has previously set a lock on that same
        -:39951:** inode.
        -:39952:**
        -:39953:** (Aside: The use of inode numbers as unique IDs does not work on VxWorks.
        -:39954:** For VxWorks, we have to use the alternative unique ID system based on
        -:39955:** canonical filename and implemented in the previous division.)
        -:39956:**
        -:39957:** The sqlite3_file structure for POSIX is no longer just an integer file
        -:39958:** descriptor.  It is now a structure that holds the integer file
        -:39959:** descriptor and a pointer to a structure that describes the internal
        -:39960:** locks on the corresponding inode.  There is one locking structure
        -:39961:** per inode, so if the same inode is opened twice, both unixFile structures
        -:39962:** point to the same locking structure.  The locking structure keeps
        -:39963:** a reference count (so we will know when to delete it) and a "cnt"
        -:39964:** field that tells us its internal lock status.  cnt==0 means the
        -:39965:** file is unlocked.  cnt==-1 means the file has an exclusive lock.
        -:39966:** cnt>0 means there are cnt shared locks on the file.
        -:39967:**
        -:39968:** Any attempt to lock or unlock a file first checks the locking
        -:39969:** structure.  The fcntl() system call is only invoked to set a
        -:39970:** POSIX lock if the internal lock structure transitions between
        -:39971:** a locked and an unlocked state.
        -:39972:**
        -:39973:** But wait:  there are yet more problems with POSIX advisory locks.
        -:39974:**
        -:39975:** If you close a file descriptor that points to a file that has locks,
        -:39976:** all locks on that file that are owned by the current process are
        -:39977:** released.  To work around this problem, each unixInodeInfo object
        -:39978:** maintains a count of the number of pending locks on the inode.
        -:39979:** When an attempt is made to close an unixFile, if there are
        -:39980:** other unixFile open on the same inode that are holding locks, the call
        -:39981:** to close() the file descriptor is deferred until all of the locks clear.
        -:39982:** The unixInodeInfo structure keeps a list of file descriptors that need to
        -:39983:** be closed and that list is walked (and cleared) when the last lock
        -:39984:** clears.
        -:39985:**
        -:39986:** Yet another problem:  LinuxThreads do not play well with posix locks.
        -:39987:**
        -:39988:** Many older versions of linux use the LinuxThreads library which is
        -:39989:** not posix compliant.  Under LinuxThreads, a lock created by thread
        -:39990:** A cannot be modified or overridden by a different thread B.
        -:39991:** Only thread A can modify the lock.  Locking behavior is correct
        -:39992:** if the application uses the newer Native Posix Thread Library (NPTL)
        -:39993:** on linux - with NPTL a lock created by thread A can override locks
        -:39994:** in thread B.  But there is no way to know at compile-time which
        -:39995:** threading library is being used.  So there is no way to know at
        -:39996:** compile-time whether or not thread A can override locks on thread B.
        -:39997:** One has to do a run-time check to discover the behavior of the
        -:39998:** current process.
        -:39999:**
        -:40000:** SQLite used to support LinuxThreads.  But support for LinuxThreads
        -:40001:** was dropped beginning with version 3.7.0.  SQLite will still work with
        -:40002:** LinuxThreads provided that (1) there is no more than one connection
        -:40003:** per database file in the same process and (2) database connections
        -:40004:** do not move across threads.
        -:40005:*/
        -:40006:
        -:40007:/*
        -:40008:** An instance of the following structure serves as the key used
        -:40009:** to locate a particular unixInodeInfo object.
        -:40010:*/
        -:40011:struct unixFileId {
        -:40012:  dev_t dev;                  /* Device number */
