        -:45795:/*
        -:45796:************************ End of sqlite3_vfs methods ***************************
        -:45797:******************************************************************************/
        -:45798:
        -:45799:/******************************************************************************
        -:45800:************************** Begin Proxy Locking ********************************
        -:45801:**
        -:45802:** Proxy locking is a "uber-locking-method" in this sense:  It uses the
        -:45803:** other locking methods on secondary lock files.  Proxy locking is a
        -:45804:** meta-layer over top of the primitive locking implemented above.  For
        -:45805:** this reason, the division that implements of proxy locking is deferred
        -:45806:** until late in the file (here) after all of the other I/O methods have
        -:45807:** been defined - so that the primitive locking methods are available
        -:45808:** as services to help with the implementation of proxy locking.
        -:45809:**
        -:45810:****
        -:45811:**
        -:45812:** The default locking schemes in SQLite use byte-range locks on the
        -:45813:** database file to coordinate safe, concurrent access by multiple readers
        -:45814:** and writers [http://sqlite.org/lockingv3.html].  The five file locking
        -:45815:** states (UNLOCKED, PENDING, SHARED, RESERVED, EXCLUSIVE) are implemented
        -:45816:** as POSIX read & write locks over fixed set of locations (via fsctl),
        -:45817:** on AFP and SMB only exclusive byte-range locks are available via fsctl
        -:45818:** with _IOWR('z', 23, struct ByteRangeLockPB2) to track the same 5 states.
        -:45819:** To simulate a F_RDLCK on the shared range, on AFP a randomly selected
        -:45820:** address in the shared range is taken for a SHARED lock, the entire
        -:45821:** shared range is taken for an EXCLUSIVE lock):
        -:45822:**
        -:45823:**      PENDING_BYTE        0x40000000
        -:45824:**      RESERVED_BYTE       0x40000001
        -:45825:**      SHARED_RANGE        0x40000002 -> 0x40000200
        -:45826:**
        -:45827:** This works well on the local file system, but shows a nearly 100x
        -:45828:** slowdown in read performance on AFP because the AFP client disables
        -:45829:** the read cache when byte-range locks are present.  Enabling the read
        -:45830:** cache exposes a cache coherency problem that is present on all OS X
        -:45831:** supported network file systems.  NFS and AFP both observe the
        -:45832:** close-to-open semantics for ensuring cache coherency
        -:45833:** [http://nfs.sourceforge.net/#faq_a8], which does not effectively
        -:45834:** address the requirements for concurrent database access by multiple
        -:45835:** readers and writers
        -:45836:** [http://www.nabble.com/SQLite-on-NFS-cache-coherency-td15655701.html].
        -:45837:**
        -:45838:** To address the performance and cache coherency issues, proxy file locking
        -:45839:** changes the way database access is controlled by limiting access to a
        -:45840:** single host at a time and moving file locks off of the database file
        -:45841:** and onto a proxy file on the local file system.
        -:45842:**
        -:45843:**
        -:45844:** Using proxy locks
        -:45845:** -----------------
        -:45846:**
        -:45847:** C APIs
        -:45848:**
        -:45849:**  sqlite3_file_control(db, dbname, SQLITE_FCNTL_SET_LOCKPROXYFILE,
        -:45850:**                       <proxy_path> | ":auto:");
        -:45851:**  sqlite3_file_control(db, dbname, SQLITE_FCNTL_GET_LOCKPROXYFILE,
        -:45852:**                       &<proxy_path>);
        -:45853:**
        -:45854:**
        -:45855:** SQL pragmas
        -:45856:**
        -:45857:**  PRAGMA [database.]lock_proxy_file=<proxy_path> | :auto:
        -:45858:**  PRAGMA [database.]lock_proxy_file
        -:45859:**
        -:45860:** Specifying ":auto:" means that if there is a conch file with a matching
        -:45861:** host ID in it, the proxy path in the conch file will be used, otherwise
        -:45862:** a proxy path based on the user's temp dir
        -:45863:** (via confstr(_CS_DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR,...)) will be used and the
        -:45864:** actual proxy file name is generated from the name and path of the
        -:45865:** database file.  For example:
        -:45866:**
        -:45867:**       For database path "/Users/me/foo.db"
        -:45868:**       The lock path will be "<tmpdir>/sqliteplocks/_Users_me_foo.db:auto:")
        -:45869:**
        -:45870:** Once a lock proxy is configured for a database connection, it can not
        -:45871:** be removed, however it may be switched to a different proxy path via
        -:45872:** the above APIs (assuming the conch file is not being held by another
        -:45873:** connection or process).
        -:45874:**
        -:45875:**
        -:45876:** How proxy locking works
        -:45877:** -----------------------
        -:45878:**
        -:45879:** Proxy file locking relies primarily on two new supporting files:
        -:45880:**
        -:45881:**   *  conch file to limit access to the database file to a single host
        -:45882:**      at a time
        -:45883:**
        -:45884:**   *  proxy file to act as a proxy for the advisory locks normally
        -:45885:**      taken on the database
        -:45886:**
        -:45887:** The conch file - to use a proxy file, sqlite must first "hold the conch"
        -:45888:** by taking an sqlite-style shared lock on the conch file, reading the
        -:45889:** contents and comparing the host's unique host ID (see below) and lock
        -:45890:** proxy path against the values stored in the conch.  The conch file is
        -:45891:** stored in the same directory as the database file and the file name
        -:45892:** is patterned after the database file name as ".<databasename>-conch".
        -:45893:** If the conch file does not exist, or its contents do not match the
        -:45894:** host ID and/or proxy path, then the lock is escalated to an exclusive
        -:45895:** lock and the conch file contents is updated with the host ID and proxy
        -:45896:** path and the lock is downgraded to a shared lock again.  If the conch
        -:45897:** is held by another process (with a shared lock), the exclusive lock
        -:45898:** will fail and SQLITE_BUSY is returned.
        -:45899:**
        -:45900:** The proxy file - a single-byte file used for all advisory file locks
        -:45901:** normally taken on the database file.   This allows for safe sharing
        -:45902:** of the database file for multiple readers and writers on the same
        -:45903:** host (the conch ensures that they all use the same local lock file).
        -:45904:**
        -:45905:** Requesting the lock proxy does not immediately take the conch, it is
        -:45906:** only taken when the first request to lock database file is made.
        -:45907:** This matches the semantics of the traditional locking behavior, where
        -:45908:** opening a connection to a database file does not take a lock on it.
        -:45909:** The shared lock and an open file descriptor are maintained until
        -:45910:** the connection to the database is closed.
        -:45911:**
        -:45912:** The proxy file and the lock file are never deleted so they only need
        -:45913:** to be created the first time they are used.
        -:45914:**
        -:45915:** Configuration options
        -:45916:** ---------------------
        -:45917:**
        -:45918:**  SQLITE_PREFER_PROXY_LOCKING
        -:45919:**
        -:45920:**       Database files accessed on non-local file systems are
        -:45921:**       automatically configured for proxy locking, lock files are
        -:45922:**       named automatically using the same logic as
        -:45923:**       PRAGMA lock_proxy_file=":auto:"
        -:45924:**
        -:45925:**  SQLITE_PROXY_DEBUG
        -:45926:**
        -:45927:**       Enables the logging of error messages during host id file
        -:45928:**       retrieval and creation
        -:45929:**
        -:45930:**  LOCKPROXYDIR
        -:45931:**
        -:45932:**       Overrides the default directory used for lock proxy files that
        -:45933:**       are named automatically via the ":auto:" setting
        -:45934:**
        -:45935:**  SQLITE_DEFAULT_PROXYDIR_PERMISSIONS
        -:45936:**
        -:45937:**       Permissions to use when creating a directory for storing the
        -:45938:**       lock proxy files, only used when LOCKPROXYDIR is not set.
        -:45939:**
        -:45940:**
        -:45941:** As mentioned above, when compiled with SQLITE_PREFER_PROXY_LOCKING,
        -:45942:** setting the environment variable SQLITE_FORCE_PROXY_LOCKING to 1 will
        -:45943:** force proxy locking to be used for every database file opened, and 0
        -:45944:** will force automatic proxy locking to be disabled for all database
        -:45945:** files (explicitly calling the SQLITE_FCNTL_SET_LOCKPROXYFILE pragma or
        -:45946:** sqlite_file_control API is not affected by SQLITE_FORCE_PROXY_LOCKING).
        -:45947:*/
        -:45948:
        -:45949:/*
        -:45950:** Proxy locking is only available on MacOSX
        -:45951:*/
        -:45952:#if defined(__APPLE__) && SQLITE_ENABLE_LOCKING_STYLE
