        -:56119:*************************************************************************
        -:56120:**
        -:56121:** This file implements the default page cache implementation (the
        -:56122:** sqlite3_pcache interface). It also contains part of the implementation
        -:56123:** of the SQLITE_CONFIG_PAGECACHE and sqlite3_release_memory() features.
        -:56124:** If the default page cache implementation is overridden, then neither of
        -:56125:** these two features are available.
        -:56126:**
        -:56127:** A Page cache line looks like this:
        -:56128:**
        -:56129:**  -------------------------------------------------------------
        -:56130:**  |  database page content   |  PgHdr1  |  MemPage  |  PgHdr  |
        -:56131:**  -------------------------------------------------------------
        -:56132:**
        -:56133:** The database page content is up front (so that buffer overreads tend to
        -:56134:** flow harmlessly into the PgHdr1, MemPage, and PgHdr extensions).   MemPage
        -:56135:** is the extension added by the btree.c module containing information such
        -:56136:** as the database page number and how that database page is used.  PgHdr
        -:56137:** is added by the pcache.c layer and contains information used to keep track
        -:56138:** of which pages are "dirty".  PgHdr1 is an extension added by this
        -:56139:** module (pcache1.c).  The PgHdr1 header is a subclass of sqlite3_pcache_page.
        -:56140:** PgHdr1 contains information needed to look up a page by its page number.
        -:56141:** The superclass sqlite3_pcache_page.pBuf points to the start of the
        -:56142:** database page content and sqlite3_pcache_page.pExtra points to PgHdr.
        -:56143:**
        -:56144:** The size of the extension (MemPage+PgHdr+PgHdr1) can be determined at
        -:56145:** runtime using sqlite3_config(SQLITE_CONFIG_PCACHE_HDRSZ, &size).  The
        -:56146:** sizes of the extensions sum to 272 bytes on x64 for 3.8.10, but this
        -:56147:** size can vary according to architecture, compile-time options, and
        -:56148:** SQLite library version number.
        -:56149:**
        -:56150:** Historical note:  It used to be that if the SQLITE_PCACHE_SEPARATE_HEADER
        -:56151:** was defined, then the page content would be held in a separate memory
        -:56152:** allocation from the PgHdr1.  This was intended to avoid clownshoe memory
        -:56153:** allocations.  However, the btree layer needs a small (16-byte) overrun
        -:56154:** area after the page content buffer.  The header serves as that overrun
        -:56155:** area.  Therefore SQLITE_PCACHE_SEPARATE_HEADER was discontinued to avoid
        -:56156:** any possibility of a memory error.
        -:56157:**
        -:56158:** This module tracks pointers to PgHdr1 objects.  Only pcache.c communicates
        -:56159:** with this module.  Information is passed back and forth as PgHdr1 pointers.
        -:56160:**
        -:56161:** The pcache.c and pager.c modules deal pointers to PgHdr objects.
        -:56162:** The btree.c module deals with pointers to MemPage objects.
        -:56163:**
        -:56164:** SOURCE OF PAGE CACHE MEMORY:
        -:56165:**
        -:56166:** Memory for a page might come from any of three sources:
        -:56167:**
        -:56168:**    (1)  The general-purpose memory allocator - sqlite3Malloc()
        -:56169:**    (2)  Global page-cache memory provided using sqlite3_config() with
        -:56170:**         SQLITE_CONFIG_PAGECACHE.
        -:56171:**    (3)  PCache-local bulk allocation.
        -:56172:**
        -:56173:** The third case is a chunk of heap memory (defaulting to 100 pages worth)
        -:56174:** that is allocated when the page cache is created.  The size of the local
        -:56175:** bulk allocation can be adjusted using
        -:56176:**
        -:56177:**     sqlite3_config(SQLITE_CONFIG_PAGECACHE, (void*)0, 0, N).
        -:56178:**
        -:56179:** If N is positive, then N pages worth of memory are allocated using a single
        -:56180:** sqlite3Malloc() call and that memory is used for the first N pages allocated.
        -:56181:** Or if N is negative, then -1024*N bytes of memory are allocated and used
        -:56182:** for as many pages as can be accommodated.
        -:56183:**
        -:56184:** Only one of (2) or (3) can be used.  Once the memory available to (2) or
        -:56185:** (3) is exhausted, subsequent allocations fail over to the general-purpose
        -:56186:** memory allocator (1).
        -:56187:**
        -:56188:** Earlier versions of SQLite used only methods (1) and (2).  But experiments
        -:56189:** show that method (3) with N==100 provides about a 5% performance boost for
        -:56190:** common workloads.
        -:56191:*/
