        -:58080:/************** End of wal.h *************************************************/
        -:58081:/************** Continuing where we left off in pager.c **********************/
        -:58082:
        -:58083:
        -:58084:/******************* NOTES ON THE DESIGN OF THE PAGER ************************
        -:58085:**
        -:58086:** This comment block describes invariants that hold when using a rollback
        -:58087:** journal.  These invariants do not apply for journal_mode=WAL,
        -:58088:** journal_mode=MEMORY, or journal_mode=OFF.
        -:58089:**
        -:58090:** Within this comment block, a page is deemed to have been synced
        -:58091:** automatically as soon as it is written when PRAGMA synchronous=OFF.
        -:58092:** Otherwise, the page is not synced until the xSync method of the VFS
        -:58093:** is called successfully on the file containing the page.
        -:58094:**
        -:58095:** Definition:  A page of the database file is said to be "overwriteable" if
        -:58096:** one or more of the following are true about the page:
        -:58097:**
        -:58098:**     (a)  The original content of the page as it was at the beginning of
        -:58099:**          the transaction has been written into the rollback journal and
        -:58100:**          synced.
        -:58101:**
        -:58102:**     (b)  The page was a freelist leaf page at the start of the transaction.
        -:58103:**
        -:58104:**     (c)  The page number is greater than the largest page that existed in
        -:58105:**          the database file at the start of the transaction.
        -:58106:**
        -:58107:** (1) A page of the database file is never overwritten unless one of the
        -:58108:**     following are true:
        -:58109:**
        -:58110:**     (a) The page and all other pages on the same sector are overwriteable.
        -:58111:**
        -:58112:**     (b) The atomic page write optimization is enabled, and the entire
        -:58113:**         transaction other than the update of the transaction sequence
        -:58114:**         number consists of a single page change.
        -:58115:**
        -:58116:** (2) The content of a page written into the rollback journal exactly matches
        -:58117:**     both the content in the database when the rollback journal was written
        -:58118:**     and the content in the database at the beginning of the current
        -:58119:**     transaction.
        -:58120:**
        -:58121:** (3) Writes to the database file are an integer multiple of the page size
        -:58122:**     in length and are aligned on a page boundary.
        -:58123:**
        -:58124:** (4) Reads from the database file are either aligned on a page boundary and
        -:58125:**     an integer multiple of the page size in length or are taken from the
        -:58126:**     first 100 bytes of the database file.
        -:58127:**
        -:58128:** (5) All writes to the database file are synced prior to the rollback journal
        -:58129:**     being deleted, truncated, or zeroed.
        -:58130:**
        -:58131:** (6) If a super-journal file is used, then all writes to the database file
        -:58132:**     are synced prior to the super-journal being deleted.
        -:58133:**
        -:58134:** Definition: Two databases (or the same database at two points it time)
        -:58135:** are said to be "logically equivalent" if they give the same answer to
        -:58136:** all queries.  Note in particular the content of freelist leaf
        -:58137:** pages can be changed arbitrarily without affecting the logical equivalence
        -:58138:** of the database.
        -:58139:**
        -:58140:** (7) At any time, if any subset, including the empty set and the total set,
        -:58141:**     of the unsynced changes to a rollback journal are removed and the
        -:58142:**     journal is rolled back, the resulting database file will be logically
        -:58143:**     equivalent to the database file at the beginning of the transaction.
        -:58144:**
        -:58145:** (8) When a transaction is rolled back, the xTruncate method of the VFS
        -:58146:**     is called to restore the database file to the same size it was at
        -:58147:**     the beginning of the transaction.  (In some VFSes, the xTruncate
        -:58148:**     method is a no-op, but that does not change the fact the SQLite will
        -:58149:**     invoke it.)
        -:58150:**
        -:58151:** (9) Whenever the database file is modified, at least one bit in the range
        -:58152:**     of bytes from 24 through 39 inclusive will be changed prior to releasing
        -:58153:**     the EXCLUSIVE lock, thus signaling other connections on the same
        -:58154:**     database to flush their caches.
        -:58155:**
        -:58156:** (10) The pattern of bits in bytes 24 through 39 shall not repeat in less
        -:58157:**      than one billion transactions.
        -:58158:**
        -:58159:** (11) A database file is well-formed at the beginning and at the conclusion
        -:58160:**      of every transaction.
        -:58161:**
        -:58162:** (12) An EXCLUSIVE lock is held on the database file when writing to
        -:58163:**      the database file.
        -:58164:**
        -:58165:** (13) A SHARED lock is held on the database file while reading any
        -:58166:**      content out of the database file.
        -:58167:**
        -:58168:******************************************************************************/
        -:58169:
        -:58170:/*
        -:58171:** Macros for troubleshooting.  Normally turned off
        -:58172:*/
        -:58173:#if 0
        -:58174:int sqlite3PagerTrace=1;  /* True to enable tracing */
        -:58175:#define sqlite3DebugPrintf printf
        -:58176:#define PAGERTRACE(X)     if( sqlite3PagerTrace ){ sqlite3DebugPrintf X; }
        -:58177:#else
        -:58178:#define PAGERTRACE(X)
        -:58179:#endif
        -:58180:
        -:58181:/*
        -:58182:** The following two macros are used within the PAGERTRACE() macros above
        -:58183:** to print out file-descriptors.
        -:58184:**
        -:58185:** PAGERID() takes a pointer to a Pager struct as its argument. The
        -:58186:** associated file-descriptor is returned. FILEHANDLEID() takes an sqlite3_file
        -:58187:** struct as its argument.
        -:58188:*/
        -:58189:#define PAGERID(p) (SQLITE_PTR_TO_INT(p->fd))
        -:58190:#define FILEHANDLEID(fd) (SQLITE_PTR_TO_INT(fd))
        -:58191:
        -:58192:/*
        -:58193:** The Pager.eState variable stores the current 'state' of a pager. A
        -:58194:** pager may be in any one of the seven states shown in the following
        -:58195:** state diagram.
        -:58196:**
        -:58197:**                            OPEN <------+------+
        -:58198:**                              |         |      |
        -:58199:**                              V         |      |
        -:58200:**               +---------> READER-------+      |
        -:58201:**               |              |                |
        -:58202:**               |              V                |
        -:58203:**               |<-------WRITER_LOCKED------> ERROR
        -:58204:**               |              |                ^
        -:58205:**               |              V                |
        -:58206:**               |<------WRITER_CACHEMOD-------->|
        -:58207:**               |              |                |
        -:58208:**               |              V                |
        -:58209:**               |<-------WRITER_DBMOD---------->|
        -:58210:**               |              |                |
        -:58211:**               |              V                |
        -:58212:**               +<------WRITER_FINISHED-------->+
        -:58213:**
        -:58214:**
        -:58215:** List of state transitions and the C [function] that performs each:
        -:58216:**
        -:58217:**   OPEN              -> READER              [sqlite3PagerSharedLock]
        -:58218:**   READER            -> OPEN                [pager_unlock]
        -:58219:**
        -:58220:**   READER            -> WRITER_LOCKED       [sqlite3PagerBegin]
        -:58221:**   WRITER_LOCKED     -> WRITER_CACHEMOD     [pager_open_journal]
        -:58222:**   WRITER_CACHEMOD   -> WRITER_DBMOD        [syncJournal]
        -:58223:**   WRITER_DBMOD      -> WRITER_FINISHED     [sqlite3PagerCommitPhaseOne]
        -:58224:**   WRITER_***        -> READER              [pager_end_transaction]
        -:58225:**
        -:58226:**   WRITER_***        -> ERROR               [pager_error]
        -:58227:**   ERROR             -> OPEN                [pager_unlock]
        -:58228:**
        -:58229:**
        -:58230:**  OPEN:
        -:58231:**
        -:58232:**    The pager starts up in this state. Nothing is guaranteed in this
        -:58233:**    state - the file may or may not be locked and the database size is
        -:58234:**    unknown. The database may not be read or written.
        -:58235:**
        -:58236:**    * No read or write transaction is active.
        -:58237:**    * Any lock, or no lock at all, may be held on the database file.
        -:58238:**    * The dbSize, dbOrigSize and dbFileSize variables may not be trusted.
        -:58239:**
        -:58240:**  READER:
        -:58241:**
        -:58242:**    In this state all the requirements for reading the database in
        -:58243:**    rollback (non-WAL) mode are met. Unless the pager is (or recently
        -:58244:**    was) in exclusive-locking mode, a user-level read transaction is
        -:58245:**    open. The database size is known in this state.
        -:58246:**
        -:58247:**    A connection running with locking_mode=normal enters this state when
        -:58248:**    it opens a read-transaction on the database and returns to state
        -:58249:**    OPEN after the read-transaction is completed. However a connection
        -:58250:**    running in locking_mode=exclusive (including temp databases) remains in
        -:58251:**    this state even after the read-transaction is closed. The only way
        -:58252:**    a locking_mode=exclusive connection can transition from READER to OPEN
        -:58253:**    is via the ERROR state (see below).
        -:58254:**
        -:58255:**    * A read transaction may be active (but a write-transaction cannot).
        -:58256:**    * A SHARED or greater lock is held on the database file.
        -:58257:**    * The dbSize variable may be trusted (even if a user-level read
        -:58258:**      transaction is not active). The dbOrigSize and dbFileSize variables
        -:58259:**      may not be trusted at this point.
        -:58260:**    * If the database is a WAL database, then the WAL connection is open.
        -:58261:**    * Even if a read-transaction is not open, it is guaranteed that
        -:58262:**      there is no hot-journal in the file-system.
        -:58263:**
        -:58264:**  WRITER_LOCKED:
        -:58265:**
        -:58266:**    The pager moves to this state from READER when a write-transaction
        -:58267:**    is first opened on the database. In WRITER_LOCKED state, all locks
        -:58268:**    required to start a write-transaction are held, but no actual
        -:58269:**    modifications to the cache or database have taken place.
        -:58270:**
        -:58271:**    In rollback mode, a RESERVED or (if the transaction was opened with
        -:58272:**    BEGIN EXCLUSIVE) EXCLUSIVE lock is obtained on the database file when
        -:58273:**    moving to this state, but the journal file is not written to or opened
        -:58274:**    to in this state. If the transaction is committed or rolled back while
        -:58275:**    in WRITER_LOCKED state, all that is required is to unlock the database
        -:58276:**    file.
        -:58277:**
        -:58278:**    IN WAL mode, WalBeginWriteTransaction() is called to lock the log file.
        -:58279:**    If the connection is running with locking_mode=exclusive, an attempt
        -:58280:**    is made to obtain an EXCLUSIVE lock on the database file.
        -:58281:**
        -:58282:**    * A write transaction is active.
        -:58283:**    * If the connection is open in rollback-mode, a RESERVED or greater
        -:58284:**      lock is held on the database file.
        -:58285:**    * If the connection is open in WAL-mode, a WAL write transaction
        -:58286:**      is open (i.e. sqlite3WalBeginWriteTransaction() has been successfully
        -:58287:**      called).
        -:58288:**    * The dbSize, dbOrigSize and dbFileSize variables are all valid.
        -:58289:**    * The contents of the pager cache have not been modified.
        -:58290:**    * The journal file may or may not be open.
        -:58291:**    * Nothing (not even the first header) has been written to the journal.
        -:58292:**
        -:58293:**  WRITER_CACHEMOD:
        -:58294:**
        -:58295:**    A pager moves from WRITER_LOCKED state to this state when a page is
        -:58296:**    first modified by the upper layer. In rollback mode the journal file
        -:58297:**    is opened (if it is not already open) and a header written to the
        -:58298:**    start of it. The database file on disk has not been modified.
        -:58299:**
        -:58300:**    * A write transaction is active.
        -:58301:**    * A RESERVED or greater lock is held on the database file.
        -:58302:**    * The journal file is open and the first header has been written
        -:58303:**      to it, but the header has not been synced to disk.
        -:58304:**    * The contents of the page cache have been modified.
        -:58305:**
        -:58306:**  WRITER_DBMOD:
        -:58307:**
        -:58308:**    The pager transitions from WRITER_CACHEMOD into WRITER_DBMOD state
        -:58309:**    when it modifies the contents of the database file. WAL connections
        -:58310:**    never enter this state (since they do not modify the database file,
        -:58311:**    just the log file).
        -:58312:**
        -:58313:**    * A write transaction is active.
        -:58314:**    * An EXCLUSIVE or greater lock is held on the database file.
        -:58315:**    * The journal file is open and the first header has been written
        -:58316:**      and synced to disk.
        -:58317:**    * The contents of the page cache have been modified (and possibly
        -:58318:**      written to disk).
        -:58319:**
        -:58320:**  WRITER_FINISHED:
        -:58321:**
        -:58322:**    It is not possible for a WAL connection to enter this state.
        -:58323:**
        -:58324:**    A rollback-mode pager changes to WRITER_FINISHED state from WRITER_DBMOD
        -:58325:**    state after the entire transaction has been successfully written into the
        -:58326:**    database file. In this state the transaction may be committed simply
        -:58327:**    by finalizing the journal file. Once in WRITER_FINISHED state, it is
        -:58328:**    not possible to modify the database further. At this point, the upper
        -:58329:**    layer must either commit or rollback the transaction.
        -:58330:**
        -:58331:**    * A write transaction is active.
        -:58332:**    * An EXCLUSIVE or greater lock is held on the database file.
        -:58333:**    * All writing and syncing of journal and database data has finished.
        -:58334:**      If no error occurred, all that remains is to finalize the journal to
        -:58335:**      commit the transaction. If an error did occur, the caller will need
        -:58336:**      to rollback the transaction.
        -:58337:**
        -:58338:**  ERROR:
        -:58339:**
        -:58340:**    The ERROR state is entered when an IO or disk-full error (including
        -:58341:**    SQLITE_IOERR_NOMEM) occurs at a point in the code that makes it
        -:58342:**    difficult to be sure that the in-memory pager state (cache contents,
        -:58343:**    db size etc.) are consistent with the contents of the file-system.
        -:58344:**
        -:58345:**    Temporary pager files may enter the ERROR state, but in-memory pagers
        -:58346:**    cannot.
        -:58347:**
        -:58348:**    For example, if an IO error occurs while performing a rollback,
        -:58349:**    the contents of the page-cache may be left in an inconsistent state.
        -:58350:**    At this point it would be dangerous to change back to READER state
        -:58351:**    (as usually happens after a rollback). Any subsequent readers might
        -:58352:**    report database corruption (due to the inconsistent cache), and if
        -:58353:**    they upgrade to writers, they may inadvertently corrupt the database
        -:58354:**    file. To avoid this hazard, the pager switches into the ERROR state
        -:58355:**    instead of READER following such an error.
        -:58356:**
        -:58357:**    Once it has entered the ERROR state, any attempt to use the pager
        -:58358:**    to read or write data returns an error. Eventually, once all
        -:58359:**    outstanding transactions have been abandoned, the pager is able to
        -:58360:**    transition back to OPEN state, discarding the contents of the
        -:58361:**    page-cache and any other in-memory state at the same time. Everything
        -:58362:**    is reloaded from disk (and, if necessary, hot-journal rollback performed)
        -:58363:**    when a read-transaction is next opened on the pager (transitioning
        -:58364:**    the pager into READER state). At that point the system has recovered
        -:58365:**    from the error.
        -:58366:**
        -:58367:**    Specifically, the pager jumps into the ERROR state if:
        -:58368:**
