        -:58370:**         function sqlite3PagerRollback().
        -:58371:**
        -:58372:**      2. An error occurs while attempting to finalize a journal file
        -:58373:**         following a commit in function sqlite3PagerCommitPhaseTwo().
        -:58374:**
        -:58375:**      3. An error occurs while attempting to write to the journal or
        -:58376:**         database file in function pagerStress() in order to free up
        -:58377:**         memory.
        -:58378:**
        -:58379:**    In other cases, the error is returned to the b-tree layer. The b-tree
        -:58380:**    layer then attempts a rollback operation. If the error condition
        -:58381:**    persists, the pager enters the ERROR state via condition (1) above.
        -:58382:**
        -:58383:**    Condition (3) is necessary because it can be triggered by a read-only
        -:58384:**    statement executed within a transaction. In this case, if the error
        -:58385:**    code were simply returned to the user, the b-tree layer would not
        -:58386:**    automatically attempt a rollback, as it assumes that an error in a
        -:58387:**    read-only statement cannot leave the pager in an internally inconsistent
        -:58388:**    state.
        -:58389:**
        -:58390:**    * The Pager.errCode variable is set to something other than SQLITE_OK.
        -:58391:**    * There are one or more outstanding references to pages (after the
        -:58392:**      last reference is dropped the pager should move back to OPEN state).
        -:58393:**    * The pager is not an in-memory pager.
        -:58394:**
        -:58395:**
        -:58396:** Notes:
        -:58397:**
        -:58398:**   * A pager is never in WRITER_DBMOD or WRITER_FINISHED state if the
        -:58399:**     connection is open in WAL mode. A WAL connection is always in one
        -:58400:**     of the first four states.
        -:58401:**
        -:58402:**   * Normally, a connection open in exclusive mode is never in PAGER_OPEN
        -:58403:**     state. There are two exceptions: immediately after exclusive-mode has
        -:58404:**     been turned on (and before any read or write transactions are
        -:58405:**     executed), and when the pager is leaving the "error state".
        -:58406:**
        -:58407:**   * See also: assert_pager_state().
        -:58408:*/
        -:58409:#define PAGER_OPEN                  0
        -:58410:#define PAGER_READER                1
        -:58411:#define PAGER_WRITER_LOCKED         2
        -:58412:#define PAGER_WRITER_CACHEMOD       3
        -:58413:#define PAGER_WRITER_DBMOD          4
        -:58414:#define PAGER_WRITER_FINISHED       5
        -:58415:#define PAGER_ERROR                 6
