        -:58508:
        -:58509:/*
        -:58510:** An open page cache is an instance of struct Pager. A description of
        -:58511:** some of the more important member variables follows:
        -:58512:**
        -:58513:** eState
        -:58514:**
        -:58515:**   The current 'state' of the pager object. See the comment and state
        -:58516:**   diagram above for a description of the pager state.
        -:58517:**
        -:58518:** eLock
        -:58519:**
        -:58520:**   For a real on-disk database, the current lock held on the database file -
        -:58521:**   NO_LOCK, SHARED_LOCK, RESERVED_LOCK or EXCLUSIVE_LOCK.
        -:58522:**
        -:58523:**   For a temporary or in-memory database (neither of which require any
        -:58524:**   locks), this variable is always set to EXCLUSIVE_LOCK. Since such
        -:58525:**   databases always have Pager.exclusiveMode==1, this tricks the pager
        -:58526:**   logic into thinking that it already has all the locks it will ever
        -:58527:**   need (and no reason to release them).
        -:58528:**
        -:58529:**   In some (obscure) circumstances, this variable may also be set to
        -:58530:**   UNKNOWN_LOCK. See the comment above the #define of UNKNOWN_LOCK for
        -:58531:**   details.
        -:58532:**
        -:58533:** changeCountDone
        -:58534:**
        -:58535:**   This boolean variable is used to make sure that the change-counter
        -:58536:**   (the 4-byte header field at byte offset 24 of the database file) is
        -:58537:**   not updated more often than necessary.
        -:58538:**
        -:58539:**   It is set to true when the change-counter field is updated, which
        -:58540:**   can only happen if an exclusive lock is held on the database file.
        -:58541:**   It is cleared (set to false) whenever an exclusive lock is
        -:58542:**   relinquished on the database file. Each time a transaction is committed,
        -:58543:**   The changeCountDone flag is inspected. If it is true, the work of
        -:58544:**   updating the change-counter is omitted for the current transaction.
        -:58545:**
        -:58546:**   This mechanism means that when running in exclusive mode, a connection
        -:58547:**   need only update the change-counter once, for the first transaction
        -:58548:**   committed.
        -:58549:**
        -:58550:** setSuper
        -:58551:**
        -:58552:**   When PagerCommitPhaseOne() is called to commit a transaction, it may
        -:58553:**   (or may not) specify a super-journal name to be written into the
        -:58554:**   journal file before it is synced to disk.
        -:58555:**
        -:58556:**   Whether or not a journal file contains a super-journal pointer affects
        -:58557:**   the way in which the journal file is finalized after the transaction is
        -:58558:**   committed or rolled back when running in "journal_mode=PERSIST" mode.
        -:58559:**   If a journal file does not contain a super-journal pointer, it is
        -:58560:**   finalized by overwriting the first journal header with zeroes. If
        -:58561:**   it does contain a super-journal pointer the journal file is finalized
        -:58562:**   by truncating it to zero bytes, just as if the connection were
        -:58563:**   running in "journal_mode=truncate" mode.
        -:58564:**
        -:58565:**   Journal files that contain super-journal pointers cannot be finalized
