        -:61624:**
        -:61625:**    OFF       sqlite3OsSync() is never called.  This is the default
        -:61626:**              for temporary and transient files.
        -:61627:**
        -:61628:**    NORMAL    The journal is synced once before writes begin on the
        -:61629:**              database.  This is normally adequate protection, but
        -:61630:**              it is theoretically possible, though very unlikely,
        -:61631:**              that an inopertune power failure could leave the journal
        -:61632:**              in a state which would cause damage to the database
        -:61633:**              when it is rolled back.
        -:61634:**
        -:61635:**    FULL      The journal is synced twice before writes begin on the
        -:61636:**              database (with some additional information - the nRec field
        -:61637:**              of the journal header - being written in between the two
        -:61638:**              syncs).  If we assume that writing a
        -:61639:**              single disk sector is atomic, then this mode provides
        -:61640:**              assurance that the journal will not be corrupted to the
        -:61641:**              point of causing damage to the database during rollback.
        -:61642:**
        -:61643:**    EXTRA     This is like FULL except that is also syncs the directory
        -:61644:**              that contains the rollback journal after the rollback
        -:61645:**              journal is unlinked.
        -:61646:**
        -:61647:** The above is for a rollback-journal mode.  For WAL mode, OFF continues
        -:61648:** to mean that no syncs ever occur.  NORMAL means that the WAL is synced
        -:61649:** prior to the start of checkpoint and that the database file is synced
        -:61650:** at the conclusion of the checkpoint if the entire content of the WAL
        -:61651:** was written back into the database.  But no sync operations occur for
        -:61652:** an ordinary commit in NORMAL mode with WAL.  FULL means that the WAL
        -:61653:** file is synced following each commit operation, in addition to the
        -:61654:** syncs associated with NORMAL.  There is no difference between FULL
        -:61655:** and EXTRA for WAL mode.
        -:61656:**
        -:61657:** Do not confuse synchronous=FULL with SQLITE_SYNC_FULL.  The
        -:61658:** SQLITE_SYNC_FULL macro means to use the MacOSX-style full-fsync
        -:61659:** using fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC).  SQLITE_SYNC_NORMAL means to do an
        -:61660:** ordinary fsync() call.  There is no difference between SQLITE_SYNC_FULL
        -:61661:** and SQLITE_SYNC_NORMAL on platforms other than MacOSX.  But the
