        -:65879:/*
        -:65880:** 2010 February 1
        -:65881:**
        -:65882:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:65883:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:65884:**
        -:65885:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:65886:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:65887:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:65888:**
        -:65889:*************************************************************************
        -:65890:**
        -:65891:** This file contains the implementation of a write-ahead log (WAL) used in
        -:65892:** "journal_mode=WAL" mode.
        -:65893:**
        -:65894:** WRITE-AHEAD LOG (WAL) FILE FORMAT
        -:65895:**
        -:65896:** A WAL file consists of a header followed by zero or more "frames".
        -:65897:** Each frame records the revised content of a single page from the
        -:65898:** database file.  All changes to the database are recorded by writing
        -:65899:** frames into the WAL.  Transactions commit when a frame is written that
        -:65900:** contains a commit marker.  A single WAL can and usually does record
        -:65901:** multiple transactions.  Periodically, the content of the WAL is
        -:65902:** transferred back into the database file in an operation called a
        -:65903:** "checkpoint".
        -:65904:**
        -:65905:** A single WAL file can be used multiple times.  In other words, the
        -:65906:** WAL can fill up with frames and then be checkpointed and then new
        -:65907:** frames can overwrite the old ones.  A WAL always grows from beginning
        -:65908:** toward the end.  Checksums and counters attached to each frame are
        -:65909:** used to determine which frames within the WAL are valid and which
        -:65910:** are leftovers from prior checkpoints.
        -:65911:**
        -:65912:** The WAL header is 32 bytes in size and consists of the following eight
        -:65913:** big-endian 32-bit unsigned integer values:
        -:65914:**
        -:65915:**     0: Magic number.  0x377f0682 or 0x377f0683
        -:65916:**     4: File format version.  Currently 3007000
        -:65917:**     8: Database page size.  Example: 1024
        -:65918:**    12: Checkpoint sequence number
        -:65919:**    16: Salt-1, random integer incremented with each checkpoint
        -:65920:**    20: Salt-2, a different random integer changing with each ckpt
        -:65921:**    24: Checksum-1 (first part of checksum for first 24 bytes of header).
        -:65922:**    28: Checksum-2 (second part of checksum for first 24 bytes of header).
        -:65923:**
        -:65924:** Immediately following the wal-header are zero or more frames. Each
        -:65925:** frame consists of a 24-byte frame-header followed by <page-size> bytes
        -:65926:** of page data. The frame-header is six big-endian 32-bit unsigned
        -:65927:** integer values, as follows:
        -:65928:**
        -:65929:**     0: Page number.
        -:65930:**     4: For commit records, the size of the database image in pages
        -:65931:**        after the commit. For all other records, zero.
        -:65932:**     8: Salt-1 (copied from the header)
        -:65933:**    12: Salt-2 (copied from the header)
        -:65934:**    16: Checksum-1.
        -:65935:**    20: Checksum-2.
        -:65936:**
        -:65937:** A frame is considered valid if and only if the following conditions are
        -:65938:** true:
        -:65939:**
        -:65940:**    (1) The salt-1 and salt-2 values in the frame-header match
        -:65941:**        salt values in the wal-header
        -:65942:**
        -:65943:**    (2) The checksum values in the final 8 bytes of the frame-header
        -:65944:**        exactly match the checksum computed consecutively on the
        -:65945:**        WAL header and the first 8 bytes and the content of all frames
        -:65946:**        up to and including the current frame.
        -:65947:**
        -:65948:** The checksum is computed using 32-bit big-endian integers if the
        -:65949:** magic number in the first 4 bytes of the WAL is 0x377f0683 and it
        -:65950:** is computed using little-endian if the magic number is 0x377f0682.
        -:65951:** The checksum values are always stored in the frame header in a
        -:65952:** big-endian format regardless of which byte order is used to compute
        -:65953:** the checksum.  The checksum is computed by interpreting the input as
        -:65954:** an even number of unsigned 32-bit integers: x[0] through x[N].  The
        -:65955:** algorithm used for the checksum is as follows:
        -:65956:**
        -:65957:**   for i from 0 to n-1 step 2:
        -:65958:**     s0 += x[i] + s1;
        -:65959:**     s1 += x[i+1] + s0;
        -:65960:**   endfor
        -:65961:**
        -:65962:** Note that s0 and s1 are both weighted checksums using fibonacci weights
        -:65963:** in reverse order (the largest fibonacci weight occurs on the first element
        -:65964:** of the sequence being summed.)  The s1 value spans all 32-bit
        -:65965:** terms of the sequence whereas s0 omits the final term.
        -:65966:**
        -:65967:** On a checkpoint, the WAL is first VFS.xSync-ed, then valid content of the
        -:65968:** WAL is transferred into the database, then the database is VFS.xSync-ed.
        -:65969:** The VFS.xSync operations serve as write barriers - all writes launched
        -:65970:** before the xSync must complete before any write that launches after the
        -:65971:** xSync begins.
        -:65972:**
        -:65973:** After each checkpoint, the salt-1 value is incremented and the salt-2
        -:65974:** value is randomized.  This prevents old and new frames in the WAL from
        -:65975:** being considered valid at the same time and being checkpointing together
        -:65976:** following a crash.
        -:65977:**
        -:65978:** READER ALGORITHM
        -:65979:**
        -:65980:** To read a page from the database (call it page number P), a reader
        -:65981:** first checks the WAL to see if it contains page P.  If so, then the
        -:65982:** last valid instance of page P that is a followed by a commit frame
        -:65983:** or is a commit frame itself becomes the value read.  If the WAL
        -:65984:** contains no copies of page P that are valid and which are a commit
        -:65985:** frame or are followed by a commit frame, then page P is read from
        -:65986:** the database file.
        -:65987:**
        -:65988:** To start a read transaction, the reader records the index of the last
        -:65989:** valid frame in the WAL.  The reader uses this recorded "mxFrame" value
        -:65990:** for all subsequent read operations.  New transactions can be appended
        -:65991:** to the WAL, but as long as the reader uses its original mxFrame value
        -:65992:** and ignores the newly appended content, it will see a consistent snapshot
        -:65993:** of the database from a single point in time.  This technique allows
        -:65994:** multiple concurrent readers to view different versions of the database
        -:65995:** content simultaneously.
        -:65996:**
        -:65997:** The reader algorithm in the previous paragraphs works correctly, but
        -:65998:** because frames for page P can appear anywhere within the WAL, the
        -:65999:** reader has to scan the entire WAL looking for page P frames.  If the
        -:66000:** WAL is large (multiple megabytes is typical) that scan can be slow,
        -:66001:** and read performance suffers.  To overcome this problem, a separate
        -:66002:** data structure called the wal-index is maintained to expedite the
        -:66003:** search for frames of a particular page.
        -:66004:**
        -:66005:** WAL-INDEX FORMAT
        -:66006:**
        -:66007:** Conceptually, the wal-index is shared memory, though VFS implementations
        -:66008:** might choose to implement the wal-index using a mmapped file.  Because
        -:66009:** the wal-index is shared memory, SQLite does not support journal_mode=WAL
        -:66010:** on a network filesystem.  All users of the database must be able to
        -:66011:** share memory.
        -:66012:**
        -:66013:** In the default unix and windows implementation, the wal-index is a mmapped
        -:66014:** file whose name is the database name with a "-shm" suffix added.  For that
        -:66015:** reason, the wal-index is sometimes called the "shm" file.
        -:66016:**
        -:66017:** The wal-index is transient.  After a crash, the wal-index can (and should
        -:66018:** be) reconstructed from the original WAL file.  In fact, the VFS is required
        -:66019:** to either truncate or zero the header of the wal-index when the last
        -:66020:** connection to it closes.  Because the wal-index is transient, it can
        -:66021:** use an architecture-specific format; it does not have to be cross-platform.
        -:66022:** Hence, unlike the database and WAL file formats which store all values
        -:66023:** as big endian, the wal-index can store multi-byte values in the native
        -:66024:** byte order of the host computer.
        -:66025:**
        -:66026:** The purpose of the wal-index is to answer this question quickly:  Given
        -:66027:** a page number P and a maximum frame index M, return the index of the
        -:66028:** last frame in the wal before frame M for page P in the WAL, or return
        -:66029:** NULL if there are no frames for page P in the WAL prior to M.
        -:66030:**
        -:66031:** The wal-index consists of a header region, followed by an one or
        -:66032:** more index blocks.
        -:66033:**
        -:66034:** The wal-index header contains the total number of frames within the WAL
        -:66035:** in the mxFrame field.
        -:66036:**
        -:66037:** Each index block except for the first contains information on
        -:66038:** HASHTABLE_NPAGE frames. The first index block contains information on
        -:66039:** HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE frames. The values of HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE and
        -:66040:** HASHTABLE_NPAGE are selected so that together the wal-index header and
        -:66041:** first index block are the same size as all other index blocks in the
        -:66042:** wal-index.  The values are:
        -:66043:**
        -:66044:**   HASHTABLE_NPAGE      4096
        -:66045:**   HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE  4062
        -:66046:**
        -:66047:** Each index block contains two sections, a page-mapping that contains the
        -:66048:** database page number associated with each wal frame, and a hash-table
        -:66049:** that allows readers to query an index block for a specific page number.
        -:66050:** The page-mapping is an array of HASHTABLE_NPAGE (or HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE
        -:66051:** for the first index block) 32-bit page numbers. The first entry in the
        -:66052:** first index-block contains the database page number corresponding to the
        -:66053:** first frame in the WAL file. The first entry in the second index block
        -:66054:** in the WAL file corresponds to the (HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE+1)th frame in
        -:66055:** the log, and so on.
        -:66056:**
        -:66057:** The last index block in a wal-index usually contains less than the full
        -:66058:** complement of HASHTABLE_NPAGE (or HASHTABLE_NPAGE_ONE) page-numbers,
        -:66059:** depending on the contents of the WAL file. This does not change the
        -:66060:** allocated size of the page-mapping array - the page-mapping array merely
        -:66061:** contains unused entries.
        -:66062:**
        -:66063:** Even without using the hash table, the last frame for page P
        -:66064:** can be found by scanning the page-mapping sections of each index block
        -:66065:** starting with the last index block and moving toward the first, and
        -:66066:** within each index block, starting at the end and moving toward the
        -:66067:** beginning.  The first entry that equals P corresponds to the frame
        -:66068:** holding the content for that page.
        -:66069:**
        -:66070:** The hash table consists of HASHTABLE_NSLOT 16-bit unsigned integers.
        -:66071:** HASHTABLE_NSLOT = 2*HASHTABLE_NPAGE, and there is one entry in the
        -:66072:** hash table for each page number in the mapping section, so the hash
        -:66073:** table is never more than half full.  The expected number of collisions
        -:66074:** prior to finding a match is 1.  Each entry of the hash table is an
        -:66075:** 1-based index of an entry in the mapping section of the same
        -:66076:** index block.   Let K be the 1-based index of the largest entry in
        -:66077:** the mapping section.  (For index blocks other than the last, K will
        -:66078:** always be exactly HASHTABLE_NPAGE (4096) and for the last index block
        -:66079:** K will be (mxFrame%HASHTABLE_NPAGE).)  Unused slots of the hash table
        -:66080:** contain a value of 0.
        -:66081:**
        -:66082:** To look for page P in the hash table, first compute a hash iKey on
        -:66083:** P as follows:
        -:66084:**
        -:66085:**      iKey = (P * 383) % HASHTABLE_NSLOT
        -:66086:**
        -:66087:** Then start scanning entries of the hash table, starting with iKey
        -:66088:** (wrapping around to the beginning when the end of the hash table is
        -:66089:** reached) until an unused hash slot is found. Let the first unused slot
        -:66090:** be at index iUnused.  (iUnused might be less than iKey if there was
        -:66091:** wrap-around.) Because the hash table is never more than half full,
        -:66092:** the search is guaranteed to eventually hit an unused entry.  Let
        -:66093:** iMax be the value between iKey and iUnused, closest to iUnused,
        -:66094:** where aHash[iMax]==P.  If there is no iMax entry (if there exists
        -:66095:** no hash slot such that aHash[i]==p) then page P is not in the
        -:66096:** current index block.  Otherwise the iMax-th mapping entry of the
        -:66097:** current index block corresponds to the last entry that references
        -:66098:** page P.
        -:66099:**
        -:66100:** A hash search begins with the last index block and moves toward the
        -:66101:** first index block, looking for entries corresponding to page P.  On
        -:66102:** average, only two or three slots in each index block need to be
        -:66103:** examined in order to either find the last entry for page P, or to
        -:66104:** establish that no such entry exists in the block.  Each index block
        -:66105:** holds over 4000 entries.  So two or three index blocks are sufficient
        -:66106:** to cover a typical 10 megabyte WAL file, assuming 1K pages.  8 or 10
        -:66107:** comparisons (on average) suffice to either locate a frame in the
        -:66108:** WAL or to establish that the frame does not exist in the WAL.  This
        -:66109:** is much faster than scanning the entire 10MB WAL.
        -:66110:**
        -:66111:** Note that entries are added in order of increasing K.  Hence, one
        -:66112:** reader might be using some value K0 and a second reader that started
        -:66113:** at a later time (after additional transactions were added to the WAL
        -:66114:** and to the wal-index) might be using a different value K1, where K1>K0.
        -:66115:** Both readers can use the same hash table and mapping section to get
        -:66116:** the correct result.  There may be entries in the hash table with
        -:66117:** K>K0 but to the first reader, those entries will appear to be unused
        -:66118:** slots in the hash table and so the first reader will get an answer as
        -:66119:** if no values greater than K0 had ever been inserted into the hash table
        -:66120:** in the first place - which is what reader one wants.  Meanwhile, the
        -:66121:** second reader using K1 will see additional values that were inserted
        -:66122:** later, which is exactly what reader two wants.
        -:66123:**
        -:66124:** When a rollback occurs, the value of K is decreased. Hash table entries
        -:66125:** that correspond to frames greater than the new K value are removed
        -:66126:** from the hash table at this point.
        -:66127:*/
        -:66128:#ifndef SQLITE_OMIT_WAL
