        -:66247:** aReadMark[K] cannot changed while there is a reader is using that mark
        -:66248:** since the reader will be holding a shared lock on WAL_READ_LOCK(K).
        -:66249:**
        -:66250:** The checkpointer may only transfer frames from WAL to database where
        -:66251:** the frame numbers are less than or equal to every aReadMark[] that is
        -:66252:** in use (that is, every aReadMark[j] for which there is a corresponding
        -:66253:** WAL_READ_LOCK(j)).  New readers (usually) pick the aReadMark[] with the
        -:66254:** largest value and will increase an unused aReadMark[] to mxFrame if there
        -:66255:** is not already an aReadMark[] equal to mxFrame.  The exception to the
        -:66256:** previous sentence is when nBackfill equals mxFrame (meaning that everything
        -:66257:** in the WAL has been backfilled into the database) then new readers
        -:66258:** will choose aReadMark[0] which has value 0 and hence such reader will
        -:66259:** get all their all content directly from the database file and ignore
        -:66260:** the WAL.
        -:66261:**
        -:66262:** Writers normally append new frames to the end of the WAL.  However,
        -:66263:** if nBackfill equals mxFrame (meaning that all WAL content has been
        -:66264:** written back into the database) and if no readers are using the WAL
        -:66265:** (in other words, if there are no WAL_READ_LOCK(i) where i>0) then
        -:66266:** the writer will first "reset" the WAL back to the beginning and start
        -:66267:** writing new content beginning at frame 1.
        -:66268:**
        -:66269:** We assume that 32-bit loads are atomic and so no locks are needed in
        -:66270:** order to read from any aReadMark[] entries.
        -:66271:*/
