        -:71144:#define PTRMAP_ISPAGE(pBt, pgno) (PTRMAP_PAGENO((pBt),(pgno))==(pgno))
        -:71145:
        -:71146:/*
        -:71147:** The pointer map is a lookup table that identifies the parent page for
        -:71148:** each child page in the database file.  The parent page is the page that
        -:71149:** contains a pointer to the child.  Every page in the database contains
        -:71150:** 0 or 1 parent pages.  (In this context 'database page' refers
        -:71151:** to any page that is not part of the pointer map itself.)  Each pointer map
        -:71152:** entry consists of a single byte 'type' and a 4 byte parent page number.
        -:71153:** The PTRMAP_XXX identifiers below are the valid types.
        -:71154:**
        -:71155:** The purpose of the pointer map is to facility moving pages from one
        -:71156:** position in the file to another as part of autovacuum.  When a page
        -:71157:** is moved, the pointer in its parent must be updated to point to the
        -:71158:** new location.  The pointer map is used to locate the parent page quickly.
        -:71159:**
        -:71160:** PTRMAP_ROOTPAGE: The database page is a root-page. The page-number is not
        -:71161:**                  used in this case.
        -:71162:**
        -:71163:** PTRMAP_FREEPAGE: The database page is an unused (free) page. The page-number
        -:71164:**                  is not used in this case.
        -:71165:**
        -:71166:** PTRMAP_OVERFLOW1: The database page is the first page in a list of
        -:71167:**                   overflow pages. The page number identifies the page that
        -:71168:**                   contains the cell with a pointer to this overflow page.
        -:71169:**
        -:71170:** PTRMAP_OVERFLOW2: The database page is the second or later page in a list of
        -:71171:**                   overflow pages. The page-number identifies the previous
        -:71172:**                   page in the overflow page list.
        -:71173:**
