        -:79008:/*
        -:79009:** A CellArray object contains a cache of pointers and sizes for a
        -:79010:** consecutive sequence of cells that might be held on multiple pages.
        -:79011:**
        -:79012:** The cells in this array are the divider cell or cells from the pParent
        -:79013:** page plus up to three child pages.  There are a total of nCell cells.
        -:79014:**
        -:79015:** pRef is a pointer to one of the pages that contributes cells.  This is
        -:79016:** used to access information such as MemPage.intKey and MemPage.pBt->pageSize
        -:79017:** which should be common to all pages that contribute cells to this array.
        -:79018:**
        -:79019:** apCell[] and szCell[] hold, respectively, pointers to the start of each
        -:79020:** cell and the size of each cell.  Some of the apCell[] pointers might refer
        -:79021:** to overflow cells.  In other words, some apCel[] pointers might not point
        -:79022:** to content area of the pages.
        -:79023:**
        -:79024:** A szCell[] of zero means the size of that cell has not yet been computed.
        -:79025:**
        -:79026:** The cells come from as many as four different pages:
        -:79027:**
        -:79028:**             -----------
        -:79029:**             | Parent  |
        -:79030:**             -----------
        -:79031:**            /     |     \
        -:79032:**           /      |      \
        -:79033:**  ---------   ---------   ---------
        -:79034:**  |Child-1|   |Child-2|   |Child-3|
        -:79035:**  ---------   ---------   ---------
        -:79036:**
        -:79037:** The order of cells is in the array is for an index btree is:
        -:79038:**
        -:79039:**       1.  All cells from Child-1 in order
        -:79040:**       2.  The first divider cell from Parent
        -:79041:**       3.  All cells from Child-2 in order
        -:79042:**       4.  The second divider cell from Parent
        -:79043:**       5.  All cells from Child-3 in order
        -:79044:**
        -:79045:** For a table-btree (with rowids) the items 2 and 4 are empty because
        -:79046:** content exists only in leaves and there are no divider cells.
        -:79047:**
        -:79048:** For an index btree, the apEnd[] array holds pointer to the end of page
        -:79049:** for Child-1, the Parent, Child-2, the Parent (again), and Child-3,
        -:79050:** respectively. The ixNx[] array holds the number of cells contained in
        -:79051:** each of these 5 stages, and all stages to the left.  Hence:
        -:79052:**
        -:79053:**    ixNx[0] = Number of cells in Child-1.
        -:79054:**    ixNx[1] = Number of cells in Child-1 plus 1 for first divider.
        -:79055:**    ixNx[2] = Number of cells in Child-1 and Child-2 + 1 for 1st divider.
        -:79056:**    ixNx[3] = Number of cells in Child-1 and Child-2 + both divider cells
        -:79057:**    ixNx[4] = Total number of cells.
        -:79058:**
        -:79059:** For a table-btree, the concept is similar, except only apEnd[0]..apEnd[2]
        -:79060:** are used and they point to the leaf pages only, and the ixNx value are:
        -:79061:**
        -:79062:**    ixNx[0] = Number of cells in Child-1.
        -:79063:**    ixNx[1] = Number of cells in Child-1 and Child-2.
        -:79064:**    ixNx[2] = Total number of cells.
        -:79065:**
        -:79066:** Sometimes when deleting, a child page can have zero cells.  In those
        -:79067:** cases, ixNx[] entries with higher indexes, and the corresponding apEnd[]
        -:79068:** entries, shift down.  The end result is that each ixNx[] entry should
        -:79069:** be larger than the previous
        -:79070:*/
