        -:104100:/*
        -:104101:** 2011-07-09
        -:104102:**
        -:104103:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:104104:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:104105:**
        -:104106:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:104107:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:104108:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:104109:**
        -:104110:*************************************************************************
        -:104111:** This file contains code for the VdbeSorter object, used in concert with
        -:104112:** a VdbeCursor to sort large numbers of keys for CREATE INDEX statements
        -:104113:** or by SELECT statements with ORDER BY clauses that cannot be satisfied
        -:104114:** using indexes and without LIMIT clauses.
        -:104115:**
        -:104116:** The VdbeSorter object implements a multi-threaded external merge sort
        -:104117:** algorithm that is efficient even if the number of elements being sorted
        -:104118:** exceeds the available memory.
        -:104119:**
        -:104120:** Here is the (internal, non-API) interface between this module and the
        -:104121:** rest of the SQLite system:
        -:104122:**
        -:104123:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterInit()       Create a new VdbeSorter object.
        -:104124:**
        -:104125:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterWrite()      Add a single new row to the VdbeSorter
        -:104126:**                                  object.  The row is a binary blob in the
        -:104127:**                                  OP_MakeRecord format that contains both
        -:104128:**                                  the ORDER BY key columns and result columns
        -:104129:**                                  in the case of a SELECT w/ ORDER BY, or
        -:104130:**                                  the complete record for an index entry
        -:104131:**                                  in the case of a CREATE INDEX.
        -:104132:**
        -:104133:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterRewind()     Sort all content previously added.
        -:104134:**                                  Position the read cursor on the
        -:104135:**                                  first sorted element.
        -:104136:**
        -:104137:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterNext()       Advance the read cursor to the next sorted
        -:104138:**                                  element.
        -:104139:**
        -:104140:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterRowkey()     Return the complete binary blob for the
        -:104141:**                                  row currently under the read cursor.
        -:104142:**
        -:104143:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterCompare()    Compare the binary blob for the row
        -:104144:**                                  currently under the read cursor against
        -:104145:**                                  another binary blob X and report if
        -:104146:**                                  X is strictly less than the read cursor.
        -:104147:**                                  Used to enforce uniqueness in a
        -:104148:**                                  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX statement.
        -:104149:**
        -:104150:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterClose()      Close the VdbeSorter object and reclaim
        -:104151:**                                  all resources.
        -:104152:**
        -:104153:**    sqlite3VdbeSorterReset()      Refurbish the VdbeSorter for reuse.  This
        -:104154:**                                  is like Close() followed by Init() only
        -:104155:**                                  much faster.
        -:104156:**
        -:104157:** The interfaces above must be called in a particular order.  Write() can
        -:104158:** only occur in between Init()/Reset() and Rewind().  Next(), Rowkey(), and
        -:104159:** Compare() can only occur in between Rewind() and Close()/Reset(). i.e.
        -:104160:**
        -:104161:**   Init()
        -:104162:**   for each record: Write()
        -:104163:**   Rewind()
        -:104164:**     Rowkey()/Compare()
        -:104165:**   Next()
        -:104166:**   Close()
        -:104167:**
        -:104168:** Algorithm:
        -:104169:**
        -:104170:** Records passed to the sorter via calls to Write() are initially held
        -:104171:** unsorted in main memory. Assuming the amount of memory used never exceeds
        -:104172:** a threshold, when Rewind() is called the set of records is sorted using
        -:104173:** an in-memory merge sort. In this case, no temporary files are required
        -:104174:** and subsequent calls to Rowkey(), Next() and Compare() read records
        -:104175:** directly from main memory.
        -:104176:**
        -:104177:** If the amount of space used to store records in main memory exceeds the
        -:104178:** threshold, then the set of records currently in memory are sorted and
        -:104179:** written to a temporary file in "Packed Memory Array" (PMA) format.
        -:104180:** A PMA created at this point is known as a "level-0 PMA". Higher levels
        -:104181:** of PMAs may be created by merging existing PMAs together - for example
        -:104182:** merging two or more level-0 PMAs together creates a level-1 PMA.
        -:104183:**
        -:104184:** The threshold for the amount of main memory to use before flushing
        -:104185:** records to a PMA is roughly the same as the limit configured for the
        -:104186:** page-cache of the main database. Specifically, the threshold is set to
        -:104187:** the value returned by "PRAGMA main.page_size" multiplied by
        -:104188:** that returned by "PRAGMA main.cache_size", in bytes.
        -:104189:**
        -:104190:** If the sorter is running in single-threaded mode, then all PMAs generated
        -:104191:** are appended to a single temporary file. Or, if the sorter is running in
        -:104192:** multi-threaded mode then up to (N+1) temporary files may be opened, where
        -:104193:** N is the configured number of worker threads. In this case, instead of
        -:104194:** sorting the records and writing the PMA to a temporary file itself, the
        -:104195:** calling thread usually launches a worker thread to do so. Except, if
        -:104196:** there are already N worker threads running, the main thread does the work
        -:104197:** itself.
        -:104198:**
        -:104199:** The sorter is running in multi-threaded mode if (a) the library was built
        -:104200:** with pre-processor symbol SQLITE_MAX_WORKER_THREADS set to a value greater
        -:104201:** than zero, and (b) worker threads have been enabled at runtime by calling
        -:104202:** "PRAGMA threads=N" with some value of N greater than 0.
        -:104203:**
        -:104204:** When Rewind() is called, any data remaining in memory is flushed to a
        -:104205:** final PMA. So at this point the data is stored in some number of sorted
        -:104206:** PMAs within temporary files on disk.
        -:104207:**
        -:104208:** If there are fewer than SORTER_MAX_MERGE_COUNT PMAs in total and the
        -:104209:** sorter is running in single-threaded mode, then these PMAs are merged
        -:104210:** incrementally as keys are retrieved from the sorter by the VDBE.  The
        -:104211:** MergeEngine object, described in further detail below, performs this
        -:104212:** merge.
        -:104213:**
        -:104214:** Or, if running in multi-threaded mode, then a background thread is
        -:104215:** launched to merge the existing PMAs. Once the background thread has
        -:104216:** merged T bytes of data into a single sorted PMA, the main thread
        -:104217:** begins reading keys from that PMA while the background thread proceeds
        -:104218:** with merging the next T bytes of data. And so on.
        -:104219:**
        -:104220:** Parameter T is set to half the value of the memory threshold used
        -:104221:** by Write() above to determine when to create a new PMA.
        -:104222:**
        -:104223:** If there are more than SORTER_MAX_MERGE_COUNT PMAs in total when
        -:104224:** Rewind() is called, then a hierarchy of incremental-merges is used.
        -:104225:** First, T bytes of data from the first SORTER_MAX_MERGE_COUNT PMAs on
        -:104226:** disk are merged together. Then T bytes of data from the second set, and
        -:104227:** so on, such that no operation ever merges more than SORTER_MAX_MERGE_COUNT
