        -:120100:/************** Begin file analyze.c *****************************************/
        -:120101:/*
        -:120102:** 2005-07-08
        -:120103:**
        -:120104:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:120105:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:120106:**
        -:120107:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:120108:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:120109:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:120110:**
        -:120111:*************************************************************************
        -:120112:** This file contains code associated with the ANALYZE command.
        -:120113:**
        -:120114:** The ANALYZE command gather statistics about the content of tables
        -:120115:** and indices.  These statistics are made available to the query planner
        -:120116:** to help it make better decisions about how to perform queries.
        -:120117:**
        -:120118:** The following system tables are or have been supported:
        -:120119:**
        -:120120:**    CREATE TABLE sqlite_stat1(tbl, idx, stat);
        -:120121:**    CREATE TABLE sqlite_stat2(tbl, idx, sampleno, sample);
        -:120122:**    CREATE TABLE sqlite_stat3(tbl, idx, nEq, nLt, nDLt, sample);
        -:120123:**    CREATE TABLE sqlite_stat4(tbl, idx, nEq, nLt, nDLt, sample);
        -:120124:**
        -:120125:** Additional tables might be added in future releases of SQLite.
        -:120126:** The sqlite_stat2 table is not created or used unless the SQLite version
        -:120127:** is between 3.6.18 and 3.7.8, inclusive, and unless SQLite is compiled
        -:120128:** with SQLITE_ENABLE_STAT2.  The sqlite_stat2 table is deprecated.
        -:120129:** The sqlite_stat2 table is superseded by sqlite_stat3, which is only
        -:120130:** created and used by SQLite versions 3.7.9 through 3.29.0 when
        -:120131:** SQLITE_ENABLE_STAT3 defined.  The functionality of sqlite_stat3
        -:120132:** is a superset of sqlite_stat2 and is also now deprecated.  The
        -:120133:** sqlite_stat4 is an enhanced version of sqlite_stat3 and is only
        -:120134:** available when compiled with SQLITE_ENABLE_STAT4 and in SQLite
        -:120135:** versions 3.8.1 and later.  STAT4 is the only variant that is still
        -:120136:** supported.
        -:120137:**
        -:120138:** For most applications, sqlite_stat1 provides all the statistics required
        -:120139:** for the query planner to make good choices.
        -:120140:**
        -:120141:** Format of sqlite_stat1:
        -:120142:**
        -:120143:** There is normally one row per index, with the index identified by the
        -:120144:** name in the idx column.  The tbl column is the name of the table to
        -:120145:** which the index belongs.  In each such row, the stat column will be
        -:120146:** a string consisting of a list of integers.  The first integer in this
        -:120147:** list is the number of rows in the index.  (This is the same as the
        -:120148:** number of rows in the table, except for partial indices.)  The second
        -:120149:** integer is the average number of rows in the index that have the same
        -:120150:** value in the first column of the index.  The third integer is the average
        -:120151:** number of rows in the index that have the same value for the first two
        -:120152:** columns.  The N-th integer (for N>1) is the average number of rows in
        -:120153:** the index which have the same value for the first N-1 columns.  For
        -:120154:** a K-column index, there will be K+1 integers in the stat column.  If
        -:120155:** the index is unique, then the last integer will be 1.
        -:120156:**
        -:120157:** The list of integers in the stat column can optionally be followed
        -:120158:** by the keyword "unordered".  The "unordered" keyword, if it is present,
        -:120159:** must be separated from the last integer by a single space.  If the
        -:120160:** "unordered" keyword is present, then the query planner assumes that
        -:120161:** the index is unordered and will not use the index for a range query.
        -:120162:**
        -:120163:** If the sqlite_stat1.idx column is NULL, then the sqlite_stat1.stat
        -:120164:** column contains a single integer which is the (estimated) number of
        -:120165:** rows in the table identified by sqlite_stat1.tbl.
        -:120166:**
        -:120167:** Format of sqlite_stat2:
        -:120168:**
        -:120169:** The sqlite_stat2 is only created and is only used if SQLite is compiled
        -:120170:** with SQLITE_ENABLE_STAT2 and if the SQLite version number is between
        -:120171:** 3.6.18 and 3.7.8.  The "stat2" table contains additional information
        -:120172:** about the distribution of keys within an index.  The index is identified by
        -:120173:** the "idx" column and the "tbl" column is the name of the table to which
        -:120174:** the index belongs.  There are usually 10 rows in the sqlite_stat2
        -:120175:** table for each index.
        -:120176:**
        -:120177:** The sqlite_stat2 entries for an index that have sampleno between 0 and 9
        -:120178:** inclusive are samples of the left-most key value in the index taken at
        -:120179:** evenly spaced points along the index.  Let the number of samples be S
        -:120180:** (10 in the standard build) and let C be the number of rows in the index.
        -:120181:** Then the sampled rows are given by:
        -:120182:**
        -:120183:**     rownumber = (i*C*2 + C)/(S*2)
        -:120184:**
        -:120185:** For i between 0 and S-1.  Conceptually, the index space is divided into
        -:120186:** S uniform buckets and the samples are the middle row from each bucket.
        -:120187:**
        -:120188:** The format for sqlite_stat2 is recorded here for legacy reference.  This
        -:120189:** version of SQLite does not support sqlite_stat2.  It neither reads nor
        -:120190:** writes the sqlite_stat2 table.  This version of SQLite only supports
        -:120191:** sqlite_stat3.
        -:120192:**
        -:120193:** Format for sqlite_stat3:
        -:120194:**
        -:120195:** The sqlite_stat3 format is a subset of sqlite_stat4.  Hence, the
        -:120196:** sqlite_stat4 format will be described first.  Further information
        -:120197:** about sqlite_stat3 follows the sqlite_stat4 description.
        -:120198:**
        -:120199:** Format for sqlite_stat4:
        -:120200:**
        -:120201:** As with sqlite_stat2, the sqlite_stat4 table contains histogram data
        -:120202:** to aid the query planner in choosing good indices based on the values
        -:120203:** that indexed columns are compared against in the WHERE clauses of
        -:120204:** queries.
        -:120205:**
        -:120206:** The sqlite_stat4 table contains multiple entries for each index.
        -:120207:** The idx column names the index and the tbl column is the table of the
        -:120208:** index.  If the idx and tbl columns are the same, then the sample is
        -:120209:** of the INTEGER PRIMARY KEY.  The sample column is a blob which is the
        -:120210:** binary encoding of a key from the index.  The nEq column is a
        -:120211:** list of integers.  The first integer is the approximate number
        -:120212:** of entries in the index whose left-most column exactly matches
        -:120213:** the left-most column of the sample.  The second integer in nEq
        -:120214:** is the approximate number of entries in the index where the
        -:120215:** first two columns match the first two columns of the sample.
        -:120216:** And so forth.  nLt is another list of integers that show the approximate
        -:120217:** number of entries that are strictly less than the sample.  The first
        -:120218:** integer in nLt contains the number of entries in the index where the
        -:120219:** left-most column is less than the left-most column of the sample.
        -:120220:** The K-th integer in the nLt entry is the number of index entries
        -:120221:** where the first K columns are less than the first K columns of the
        -:120222:** sample.  The nDLt column is like nLt except that it contains the
        -:120223:** number of distinct entries in the index that are less than the
        -:120224:** sample.
        -:120225:**
        -:120226:** There can be an arbitrary number of sqlite_stat4 entries per index.
        -:120227:** The ANALYZE command will typically generate sqlite_stat4 tables
        -:120228:** that contain between 10 and 40 samples which are distributed across
        -:120229:** the key space, though not uniformly, and which include samples with
        -:120230:** large nEq values.
        -:120231:**
        -:120232:** Format for sqlite_stat3 redux:
        -:120233:**
        -:120234:** The sqlite_stat3 table is like sqlite_stat4 except that it only
        -:120235:** looks at the left-most column of the index.  The sqlite_stat3.sample
        -:120236:** column contains the actual value of the left-most column instead
        -:120237:** of a blob encoding of the complete index key as is found in
        -:120238:** sqlite_stat4.sample.  The nEq, nLt, and nDLt entries of sqlite_stat3
        -:120239:** all contain just a single integer which is the same as the first
        -:120240:** integer in the equivalent columns in sqlite_stat4.
        -:120241:*/
        -:120242:#ifndef SQLITE_OMIT_ANALYZE
        -:120243:/* #include "sqliteInt.h" */
        -:120244:
        -:120245:#if defined(SQLITE_ENABLE_STAT4)
        -:120246:# define IsStat4     1
        -:120247:#else
        -:120248:# define IsStat4     0
        -:120249:# undef SQLITE_STAT4_SAMPLES
        -:120250:# define SQLITE_STAT4_SAMPLES 1
        -:120251:#endif
