        -:133345:
        -:133346:/*
        -:133347:** Deferred and Immediate FKs
        -:133348:** --------------------------
        -:133349:**
        -:133350:** Foreign keys in SQLite come in two flavours: deferred and immediate.
        -:133351:** If an immediate foreign key constraint is violated,
        -:133352:** SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_FOREIGNKEY is returned and the current
        -:133353:** statement transaction rolled back. If a
        -:133354:** deferred foreign key constraint is violated, no action is taken
        -:133355:** immediately. However if the application attempts to commit the
        -:133356:** transaction before fixing the constraint violation, the attempt fails.
        -:133357:**
        -:133358:** Deferred constraints are implemented using a simple counter associated
        -:133359:** with the database handle. The counter is set to zero each time a
        -:133360:** database transaction is opened. Each time a statement is executed
        -:133361:** that causes a foreign key violation, the counter is incremented. Each
        -:133362:** time a statement is executed that removes an existing violation from
        -:133363:** the database, the counter is decremented. When the transaction is
        -:133364:** committed, the commit fails if the current value of the counter is
        -:133365:** greater than zero. This scheme has two big drawbacks:
        -:133366:**
        -:133367:**   * When a commit fails due to a deferred foreign key constraint,
        -:133368:**     there is no way to tell which foreign constraint is not satisfied,
        -:133369:**     or which row it is not satisfied for.
        -:133370:**
        -:133371:**   * If the database contains foreign key violations when the
        -:133372:**     transaction is opened, this may cause the mechanism to malfunction.
        -:133373:**
        -:133374:** Despite these problems, this approach is adopted as it seems simpler
        -:133375:** than the alternatives.
        -:133376:**
        -:133377:** INSERT operations:
        -:133378:**
        -:133379:**   I.1) For each FK for which the table is the child table, search
        -:133380:**        the parent table for a match. If none is found increment the
        -:133381:**        constraint counter.
        -:133382:**
        -:133383:**   I.2) For each FK for which the table is the parent table,
        -:133384:**        search the child table for rows that correspond to the new
        -:133385:**        row in the parent table. Decrement the counter for each row
        -:133386:**        found (as the constraint is now satisfied).
        -:133387:**
        -:133388:** DELETE operations:
        -:133389:**
        -:133390:**   D.1) For each FK for which the table is the child table,
        -:133391:**        search the parent table for a row that corresponds to the
        -:133392:**        deleted row in the child table. If such a row is not found,
        -:133393:**        decrement the counter.
        -:133394:**
        -:133395:**   D.2) For each FK for which the table is the parent table, search
        -:133396:**        the child table for rows that correspond to the deleted row
        -:133397:**        in the parent table. For each found increment the counter.
        -:133398:**
        -:133399:** UPDATE operations:
        -:133400:**
        -:133401:**   An UPDATE command requires that all 4 steps above are taken, but only
        -:133402:**   for FK constraints for which the affected columns are actually
        -:133403:**   modified (values must be compared at runtime).
        -:133404:**
        -:133405:** Note that I.1 and D.1 are very similar operations, as are I.2 and D.2.
        -:133406:** This simplifies the implementation a bit.
        -:133407:**
        -:133408:** For the purposes of immediate FK constraints, the OR REPLACE conflict
        -:133409:** resolution is considered to delete rows before the new row is inserted.
        -:133410:** If a delete caused by OR REPLACE violates an FK constraint, an exception
        -:133411:** is thrown, even if the FK constraint would be satisfied after the new
        -:133412:** row is inserted.
        -:133413:**
        -:133414:** Immediate constraints are usually handled similarly. The only difference
        -:133415:** is that the counter used is stored as part of each individual statement
        -:133416:** object (struct Vdbe). If, after the statement has run, its immediate
        -:133417:** constraint counter is greater than zero,
        -:133418:** it returns SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_FOREIGNKEY
        -:133419:** and the statement transaction is rolled back. An exception is an INSERT
        -:133420:** statement that inserts a single row only (no triggers). In this case,
        -:133421:** instead of using a counter, an exception is thrown immediately if the
        -:133422:** INSERT violates a foreign key constraint. This is necessary as such
        -:133423:** an INSERT does not open a statement transaction.
        -:133424:**
        -:133425:** TODO: How should dropping a table be handled? How should renaming a
        -:133426:** table be handled?
        -:133427:**
        -:133428:**
        -:133429:** Query API Notes
        -:133430:** ---------------
        -:133431:**
        -:133432:** Before coding an UPDATE or DELETE row operation, the code-generator
        -:133433:** for those two operations needs to know whether or not the operation
        -:133434:** requires any FK processing and, if so, which columns of the original
        -:133435:** row are required by the FK processing VDBE code (i.e. if FKs were
        -:133436:** implemented using triggers, which of the old.* columns would be
        -:133437:** accessed). No information is required by the code-generator before
        -:133438:** coding an INSERT operation. The functions used by the UPDATE/DELETE
        -:133439:** generation code to query for this information are:
        -:133440:**
        -:133441:**   sqlite3FkRequired() - Test to see if FK processing is required.
        -:133442:**   sqlite3FkOldmask()  - Query for the set of required old.* columns.
        -:133443:**
        -:133444:**
        -:133445:** Externally accessible module functions
        -:133446:** --------------------------------------
        -:133447:**
        -:133448:**   sqlite3FkCheck()    - Check for foreign key violations.
        -:133449:**   sqlite3FkActions()  - Code triggers for ON UPDATE/ON DELETE actions.
        -:133450:**   sqlite3FkDelete()   - Delete an FKey structure.
        -:133451:*/
        -:133452:
        -:133453:/*
        -:133454:** VDBE Calling Convention
        -:133455:** -----------------------
        -:133456:**
        -:133457:** Example:
        -:133458:**
        -:133459:**   For the following INSERT statement:
        -:133460:**
        -:133461:**     CREATE TABLE t1(a, b INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, c);
        -:133462:**     INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(1, 2, 3.1);
        -:133463:**
        -:133464:**   Register (x):        2    (type integer)
        -:133465:**   Register (x+1):      1    (type integer)
        -:133466:**   Register (x+2):      NULL (type NULL)
        -:133467:**   Register (x+3):      3.1  (type real)
        -:133468:*/
        -:133469:
        -:133470:/*
        -:133471:** A foreign key constraint requires that the key columns in the parent
        -:133472:** table are collectively subject to a UNIQUE or PRIMARY KEY constraint.
        -:133473:** Given that pParent is the parent table for foreign key constraint pFKey,
        -:133474:** search the schema for a unique index on the parent key columns.
        -:133475:**
        -:133476:** If successful, zero is returned. If the parent key is an INTEGER PRIMARY
        -:133477:** KEY column, then output variable *ppIdx is set to NULL. Otherwise, *ppIdx
        -:133478:** is set to point to the unique index.
        -:133479:**
        -:133480:** If the parent key consists of a single column (the foreign key constraint
        -:133481:** is not a composite foreign key), output variable *paiCol is set to NULL.
        -:133482:** Otherwise, it is set to point to an allocated array of size N, where
        -:133483:** N is the number of columns in the parent key. The first element of the
        -:133484:** array is the index of the child table column that is mapped by the FK
        -:133485:** constraint to the parent table column stored in the left-most column
        -:133486:** of index *ppIdx. The second element of the array is the index of the
        -:133487:** child table column that corresponds to the second left-most column of
        -:133488:** *ppIdx, and so on.
        -:133489:**
        -:133490:** If the required index cannot be found, either because:
        -:133491:**
        -:133492:**   1) The named parent key columns do not exist, or
        -:133493:**
        -:133494:**   2) The named parent key columns do exist, but are not subject to a
        -:133495:**      UNIQUE or PRIMARY KEY constraint, or
        -:133496:**
        -:133497:**   3) No parent key columns were provided explicitly as part of the
        -:133498:**      foreign key definition, and the parent table does not have a
        -:133499:**      PRIMARY KEY, or
        -:133500:**
        -:133501:**   4) No parent key columns were provided explicitly as part of the
        -:133502:**      foreign key definition, and the PRIMARY KEY of the parent table
        -:133503:**      consists of a different number of columns to the child key in
        -:133504:**      the child table.
        -:133505:**
        -:133506:** then non-zero is returned, and a "foreign key mismatch" error loaded
        -:133507:** into pParse. If an OOM error occurs, non-zero is returned and the
        -:133508:** pParse->db->mallocFailed flag is set.
        -:133509:*/
