        -:140701:/*
        -:140702:** When the 0x10 bit of PRAGMA optimize is set, any ANALYZE commands
        -:140703:** will be run with an analysis_limit set to the lessor of the value of
        -:140704:** the following macro or to the actual analysis_limit if it is non-zero,
        -:140705:** in order to prevent PRAGMA optimize from running for too long.
        -:140706:**
        -:140707:** The value of 2000 is chosen empirically so that the worst-case run-time
        -:140708:** for PRAGMA optimize does not exceed 100 milliseconds against a variety
        -:140709:** of test databases on a RaspberryPI-4 compiled using -Os and without
        -:140710:** -DSQLITE_DEBUG.  Of course, your mileage may vary.  For the purpose of
        -:140711:** this paragraph, "worst-case" means that ANALYZE ends up being
        -:140712:** run on every table in the database.  The worst case typically only
        -:140713:** happens if PRAGMA optimize is run on a database file for which ANALYZE
        -:140714:** has not been previously run and the 0x10000 flag is included so that
        -:140715:** all tables are analyzed.  The usual case for PRAGMA optimize is that
        -:140716:** no ANALYZE commands will be run at all, or if any ANALYZE happens it
        -:140717:** will be against a single table, so that expected timing for PRAGMA
        -:140718:** optimize on a PI-4 is more like 1 millisecond or less with the 0x10000
        -:140719:** flag or less than 100 microseconds without the 0x10000 flag.
        -:140720:**
        -:140721:** An analysis limit of 2000 is almost always sufficient for the query
        -:140722:** planner to fully characterize an index.  The additional accuracy from
        -:140723:** a larger analysis is not usually helpful.
        -:140724:*/
        -:140725:#ifndef SQLITE_DEFAULT_OPTIMIZE_LIMIT
        -:140726:# define SQLITE_DEFAULT_OPTIMIZE_LIMIT 2000
        -:140727:#endif
