        -:149045:#if !defined(SQLITE_OMIT_SUBQUERY) || !defined(SQLITE_OMIT_VIEW)
        -:149046:/*
        -:149047:** This routine attempts to flatten subqueries as a performance optimization.
        -:149048:** This routine returns 1 if it makes changes and 0 if no flattening occurs.
        -:149049:**
        -:149050:** To understand the concept of flattening, consider the following
        -:149051:** query:
        -:149052:**
        -:149053:**     SELECT a FROM (SELECT x+y AS a FROM t1 WHERE z<100) WHERE a>5
        -:149054:**
        -:149055:** The default way of implementing this query is to execute the
        -:149056:** subquery first and store the results in a temporary table, then
        -:149057:** run the outer query on that temporary table.  This requires two
        -:149058:** passes over the data.  Furthermore, because the temporary table
        -:149059:** has no indices, the WHERE clause on the outer query cannot be
        -:149060:** optimized.
        -:149061:**
        -:149062:** This routine attempts to rewrite queries such as the above into
        -:149063:** a single flat select, like this:
        -:149064:**
        -:149065:**     SELECT x+y AS a FROM t1 WHERE z<100 AND a>5
        -:149066:**
        -:149067:** The code generated for this simplification gives the same result
        -:149068:** but only has to scan the data once.  And because indices might
        -:149069:** exist on the table t1, a complete scan of the data might be
        -:149070:** avoided.
        -:149071:**
        -:149072:** Flattening is subject to the following constraints:
        -:149073:**
        -:149074:**  (**)  We no longer attempt to flatten aggregate subqueries. Was:
        -:149075:**        The subquery and the outer query cannot both be aggregates.
        -:149076:**
        -:149077:**  (**)  We no longer attempt to flatten aggregate subqueries. Was:
        -:149078:**        (2) If the subquery is an aggregate then
        -:149079:**        (2a) the outer query must not be a join and
        -:149080:**        (2b) the outer query must not use subqueries
        -:149081:**             other than the one FROM-clause subquery that is a candidate
        -:149082:**             for flattening.  (This is due to ticket [2f7170d73bf9abf80]
        -:149083:**             from 2015-02-09.)
        -:149084:**
        -:149085:**   (3)  If the subquery is the right operand of a LEFT JOIN then
        -:149086:**        (3a) the subquery may not be a join
        -:149087:**        (**) Was (3b): "the FROM clause of the subquery may not contain
        -:149088:**             a virtual table"
        -:149089:**        (**) Was: "The outer query may not have a GROUP BY." This case
        -:149090:**             is now managed correctly
        -:149091:**        (3d) the outer query may not be DISTINCT.
        -:149092:**        See also (26) for restrictions on RIGHT JOIN.
        -:149093:**
        -:149094:**   (4)  The subquery can not be DISTINCT.
        -:149095:**
        -:149096:**  (**)  At one point restrictions (4) and (5) defined a subset of DISTINCT
        -:149097:**        sub-queries that were excluded from this optimization. Restriction
        -:149098:**        (4) has since been expanded to exclude all DISTINCT subqueries.
        -:149099:**
        -:149100:**  (**)  We no longer attempt to flatten aggregate subqueries.  Was:
        -:149101:**        If the subquery is aggregate, the outer query may not be DISTINCT.
        -:149102:**
        -:149103:**   (7)  The subquery must have a FROM clause.  TODO:  For subqueries without
        -:149104:**        A FROM clause, consider adding a FROM clause with the special
        -:149105:**        table sqlite_once that consists of a single row containing a
        -:149106:**        single NULL.
        -:149107:**
        -:149108:**   (8)  If the subquery uses LIMIT then the outer query may not be a join.
        -:149109:**
        -:149110:**   (9)  If the subquery uses LIMIT then the outer query may not be aggregate.
        -:149111:**
        -:149112:**  (**)  Restriction (10) was removed from the code on 2005-02-05 but we
        -:149113:**        accidentally carried the comment forward until 2014-09-15.  Original
        -:149114:**        constraint: "If the subquery is aggregate then the outer query
        -:149115:**        may not use LIMIT."
        -:149116:**
        -:149117:**  (11)  The subquery and the outer query may not both have ORDER BY clauses.
        -:149118:**
        -:149119:**  (**)  Not implemented.  Subsumed into restriction (3).  Was previously
        -:149120:**        a separate restriction deriving from ticket #350.
        -:149121:**
        -:149122:**  (13)  The subquery and outer query may not both use LIMIT.
        -:149123:**
        -:149124:**  (14)  The subquery may not use OFFSET.
        -:149125:**
        -:149126:**  (15)  If the outer query is part of a compound select, then the
        -:149127:**        subquery may not use LIMIT.
        -:149128:**        (See ticket #2339 and ticket [02a8e81d44]).
        -:149129:**
        -:149130:**  (16)  If the outer query is aggregate, then the subquery may not
        -:149131:**        use ORDER BY.  (Ticket #2942)  This used to not matter
        -:149132:**        until we introduced the group_concat() function.
        -:149133:**
        -:149134:**  (17)  If the subquery is a compound select, then
        -:149135:**        (17a) all compound operators must be a UNION ALL, and
        -:149136:**        (17b) no terms within the subquery compound may be aggregate
        -:149137:**              or DISTINCT, and
        -:149138:**        (17c) every term within the subquery compound must have a FROM clause
        -:149139:**        (17d) the outer query may not be
        -:149140:**              (17d1) aggregate, or
        -:149141:**              (17d2) DISTINCT
        -:149142:**        (17e) the subquery may not contain window functions, and
        -:149143:**        (17f) the subquery must not be the RHS of a LEFT JOIN.
        -:149144:**        (17g) either the subquery is the first element of the outer
        -:149145:**              query or there are no RIGHT or FULL JOINs in any arm
        -:149146:**              of the subquery.  (This is a duplicate of condition (27b).)
        -:149147:**        (17h) The corresponding result set expressions in all arms of the
        -:149148:**              compound must have the same affinity.
        -:149149:**
        -:149150:**        The parent and sub-query may contain WHERE clauses. Subject to
        -:149151:**        rules (11), (13) and (14), they may also contain ORDER BY,
        -:149152:**        LIMIT and OFFSET clauses.  The subquery cannot use any compound
        -:149153:**        operator other than UNION ALL because all the other compound
        -:149154:**        operators have an implied DISTINCT which is disallowed by
        -:149155:**        restriction (4).
        -:149156:**
        -:149157:**        Also, each component of the sub-query must return the same number
        -:149158:**        of result columns. This is actually a requirement for any compound
        -:149159:**        SELECT statement, but all the code here does is make sure that no
        -:149160:**        such (illegal) sub-query is flattened. The caller will detect the
        -:149161:**        syntax error and return a detailed message.
        -:149162:**
        -:149163:**  (18)  If the sub-query is a compound select, then all terms of the
        -:149164:**        ORDER BY clause of the parent must be copies of a term returned
        -:149165:**        by the parent query.
        -:149166:**
        -:149167:**  (19)  If the subquery uses LIMIT then the outer query may not
        -:149168:**        have a WHERE clause.
        -:149169:**
        -:149170:**  (20)  If the sub-query is a compound select, then it must not use
        -:149171:**        an ORDER BY clause.  Ticket #3773.  We could relax this constraint
        -:149172:**        somewhat by saying that the terms of the ORDER BY clause must
        -:149173:**        appear as unmodified result columns in the outer query.  But we
        -:149174:**        have other optimizations in mind to deal with that case.
        -:149175:**
        -:149176:**  (21)  If the subquery uses LIMIT then the outer query may not be
        -:149177:**        DISTINCT.  (See ticket [752e1646fc]).
        -:149178:**
        -:149179:**  (22)  The subquery may not be a recursive CTE.
        -:149180:**
        -:149181:**  (23)  If the outer query is a recursive CTE, then the sub-query may not be
        -:149182:**        a compound query.  This restriction is because transforming the
        -:149183:**        parent to a compound query confuses the code that handles
        -:149184:**        recursive queries in multiSelect().
        -:149185:**
        -:149186:**  (**)  We no longer attempt to flatten aggregate subqueries.  Was:
        -:149187:**        The subquery may not be an aggregate that uses the built-in min() or
        -:149188:**        or max() functions.  (Without this restriction, a query like:
        -:149189:**        "SELECT x FROM (SELECT max(y), x FROM t1)" would not necessarily
        -:149190:**        return the value X for which Y was maximal.)
        -:149191:**
        -:149192:**  (25)  If either the subquery or the parent query contains a window
        -:149193:**        function in the select list or ORDER BY clause, flattening
        -:149194:**        is not attempted.
        -:149195:**
        -:149196:**  (26)  The subquery may not be the right operand of a RIGHT JOIN.
        -:149197:**        See also (3) for restrictions on LEFT JOIN.
        -:149198:**
        -:149199:**  (27)  The subquery may not contain a FULL or RIGHT JOIN unless it
        -:149200:**        is the first element of the parent query.  Two subcases:
        -:149201:**        (27a) the subquery is not a compound query.
        -:149202:**        (27b) the subquery is a compound query and the RIGHT JOIN occurs
        -:149203:**              in any arm of the compound query.  (See also (17g).)
        -:149204:**
        -:149205:**  (28)  The subquery is not a MATERIALIZED CTE.  (This is handled
        -:149206:**        in the caller before ever reaching this routine.)
        -:149207:**
        -:149208:**
        -:149209:** In this routine, the "p" parameter is a pointer to the outer query.
        -:149210:** The subquery is p->pSrc->a[iFrom].  isAgg is true if the outer query
