        -:149844:** For example, the query:
        -:149845:**
        -:149846:**      SELECT * FROM t1, t2, t3 WHERE t1.a=39 AND t2.b=t1.a AND t3.c=t2.b
        -:149847:**
        -:149848:** Is transformed into
        -:149849:**
        -:149850:**      SELECT * FROM t1, t2, t3 WHERE t1.a=39 AND t2.b=39 AND t3.c=39
        -:149851:**
        -:149852:** Return true if any transformations where made and false if not.
        -:149853:**
        -:149854:** Implementation note:  Constant propagation is tricky due to affinity
        -:149855:** and collating sequence interactions.  Consider this example:
        -:149856:**
        -:149857:**    CREATE TABLE t1(a INT,b TEXT);
        -:149858:**    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(123,'0123');
        -:149859:**    SELECT * FROM t1 WHERE a=123 AND b=a;
        -:149860:**    SELECT * FROM t1 WHERE a=123 AND b=123;
        -:149861:**
        -:149862:** The two SELECT statements above should return different answers.  b=a
        -:149863:** is always true because the comparison uses numeric affinity, but b=123
        -:149864:** is false because it uses text affinity and '0123' is not the same as '123'.
        -:149865:** To work around this, the expression tree is not actually changed from
        -:149866:** "b=a" to "b=123" but rather the "a" in "b=a" is tagged with EP_FixedCol
        -:149867:** and the "123" value is hung off of the pLeft pointer.  Code generator
        -:149868:** routines know to generate the constant "123" instead of looking up the
        -:149869:** column value.  Also, to avoid collation problems, this optimization is
        -:149870:** only attempted if the "a=123" term uses the default BINARY collation.
        -:149871:**
        -:149872:** 2021-05-25 forum post 6a06202608: Another troublesome case is...
        -:149873:**
        -:149874:**    CREATE TABLE t1(x);
        -:149875:**    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(10.0);
        -:149876:**    SELECT 1 FROM t1 WHERE x=10 AND x LIKE 10;
