        -:149969:**
        -:149970:** The hope is that the terms added to the inner query will make it more
        -:149971:** efficient.
        -:149972:**
        -:149973:** NAME AMBIGUITY
        -:149974:**
        -:149975:** This optimization is called the "WHERE-clause push-down optimization"
        -:149976:** or sometimes the "predicate push-down optimization".
        -:149977:**
        -:149978:** Do not confuse this optimization with another unrelated optimization
        -:149979:** with a similar name:  The "MySQL push-down optimization" causes WHERE
        -:149980:** clause terms that can be evaluated using only the index and without
        -:149981:** reference to the table are run first, so that if they are false,
        -:149982:** unnecessary table seeks are avoided.
        -:149983:**
        -:149984:** RULES
        -:149985:**
        -:149986:** Do not attempt this optimization if:
        -:149987:**
        -:149988:**   (1) (** This restriction was removed on 2017-09-29.  We used to
        -:149989:**           disallow this optimization for aggregate subqueries, but now
        -:149990:**           it is allowed by putting the extra terms on the HAVING clause.
        -:149991:**           The added HAVING clause is pointless if the subquery lacks
        -:149992:**           a GROUP BY clause.  But such a HAVING clause is also harmless
        -:149993:**           so there does not appear to be any reason to add extra logic
        -:149994:**           to suppress it. **)
        -:149995:**
        -:149996:**   (2) The inner query is the recursive part of a common table expression.
        -:149997:**
        -:149998:**   (3) The inner query has a LIMIT clause (since the changes to the WHERE
        -:149999:**       clause would change the meaning of the LIMIT).
        -:150000:**
        -:150001:**   (4) The inner query is the right operand of a LEFT JOIN and the
        -:150002:**       expression to be pushed down does not come from the ON clause
        -:150003:**       on that LEFT JOIN.
        -:150004:**
        -:150005:**   (5) The WHERE clause expression originates in the ON or USING clause
        -:150006:**       of a LEFT JOIN where iCursor is not the right-hand table of that
        -:150007:**       left join.  An example:
        -:150008:**
        -:150009:**           SELECT *
        -:150010:**           FROM (SELECT 1 AS a1 UNION ALL SELECT 2) AS aa
        -:150011:**           JOIN (SELECT 1 AS b2 UNION ALL SELECT 2) AS bb ON (a1=b2)
        -:150012:**           LEFT JOIN (SELECT 8 AS c3 UNION ALL SELECT 9) AS cc ON (b2=2);
        -:150013:**
        -:150014:**       The correct answer is three rows:  (1,1,NULL),(2,2,8),(2,2,9).
        -:150015:**       But if the (b2=2) term were to be pushed down into the bb subquery,
        -:150016:**       then the (1,1,NULL) row would be suppressed.
        -:150017:**
        -:150018:**   (6) Window functions make things tricky as changes to the WHERE clause
        -:150019:**       of the inner query could change the window over which window
        -:150020:**       functions are calculated. Therefore, do not attempt the optimization
        -:150021:**       if:
        -:150022:**
        -:150023:**     (6a) The inner query uses multiple incompatible window partitions.
        -:150024:**
        -:150025:**     (6b) The inner query is a compound and uses window-functions.
        -:150026:**
        -:150027:**     (6c) The WHERE clause does not consist entirely of constants and
        -:150028:**          copies of expressions found in the PARTITION BY clause of
        -:150029:**          all window-functions used by the sub-query. It is safe to
        -:150030:**          filter out entire partitions, as this does not change the
        -:150031:**          window over which any window-function is calculated.
        -:150032:**
        -:150033:**   (7) The inner query is a Common Table Expression (CTE) that should
        -:150034:**       be materialized.  (This restriction is implemented in the calling
        -:150035:**       routine.)
        -:150036:**
        -:150037:**   (8) If the subquery is a compound that uses UNION, INTERSECT,
        -:150038:**       or EXCEPT, then all of the result set columns for all arms of
        -:150039:**       the compound must use the BINARY collating sequence.
        -:150040:**
        -:150041:**   (9) All three of the following are true:
        -:150042:**
        -:150043:**       (9a) The WHERE clause expression originates in the ON or USING clause
