        -:159148:
        -:159149:/*
        -:159150:** Each instance of this object holds a sequence of WhereLoop objects
        -:159151:** that implement some or all of a query plan.
        -:159152:**
        -:159153:** Think of each WhereLoop object as a node in a graph with arcs
        -:159154:** showing dependencies and costs for travelling between nodes.  (That is
        -:159155:** not a completely accurate description because WhereLoop costs are a
        -:159156:** vector, not a scalar, and because dependencies are many-to-one, not
        -:159157:** one-to-one as are graph nodes.  But it is a useful visualization aid.)
        -:159158:** Then a WherePath object is a path through the graph that visits some
        -:159159:** or all of the WhereLoop objects once.
        -:159160:**
        -:159161:** The "solver" works by creating the N best WherePath objects of length
        -:159162:** 1.  Then using those as a basis to compute the N best WherePath objects
        -:159163:** of length 2.  And so forth until the length of WherePaths equals the
