        -:163123:#if !defined(SQLITE_OMIT_OR_OPTIMIZATION) && !defined(SQLITE_OMIT_SUBQUERY)
        -:163124:/*
        -:163125:** Analyze a term that consists of two or more OR-connected
        -:163126:** subterms.  So in:
        -:163127:**
        -:163128:**     ... WHERE  (a=5) AND (b=7 OR c=9 OR d=13) AND (d=13)
        -:163129:**                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
        -:163130:**
        -:163131:** This routine analyzes terms such as the middle term in the above example.
        -:163132:** A WhereOrTerm object is computed and attached to the term under
        -:163133:** analysis, regardless of the outcome of the analysis.  Hence:
        -:163134:**
        -:163135:**     WhereTerm.wtFlags   |=  TERM_ORINFO
        -:163136:**     WhereTerm.u.pOrInfo  =  a dynamically allocated WhereOrTerm object
        -:163137:**
        -:163138:** The term being analyzed must have two or more of OR-connected subterms.
        -:163139:** A single subterm might be a set of AND-connected sub-subterms.
        -:163140:** Examples of terms under analysis:
        -:163141:**
        -:163142:**     (A)     t1.x=t2.y OR t1.x=t2.z OR t1.y=15 OR t1.z=t3.a+5
        -:163143:**     (B)     x=expr1 OR expr2=x OR x=expr3
        -:163144:**     (C)     t1.x=t2.y OR (t1.x=t2.z AND t1.y=15)
        -:163145:**     (D)     x=expr1 OR (y>11 AND y<22 AND z LIKE '*hello*')
        -:163146:**     (E)     (p.a=1 AND q.b=2 AND r.c=3) OR (p.x=4 AND q.y=5 AND r.z=6)
        -:163147:**     (F)     x>A OR (x=A AND y>=B)
        -:163148:**
        -:163149:** CASE 1:
        -:163150:**
        -:163151:** If all subterms are of the form T.C=expr for some single column of C and
        -:163152:** a single table T (as shown in example B above) then create a new virtual
        -:163153:** term that is an equivalent IN expression.  In other words, if the term
        -:163154:** being analyzed is:
        -:163155:**
        -:163156:**      x = expr1  OR  expr2 = x  OR  x = expr3
        -:163157:**
        -:163158:** then create a new virtual term like this:
        -:163159:**
        -:163160:**      x IN (expr1,expr2,expr3)
        -:163161:**
        -:163162:** CASE 2:
        -:163163:**
        -:163164:** If there are exactly two disjuncts and one side has x>A and the other side
        -:163165:** has x=A (for the same x and A) then add a new virtual conjunct term to the
        -:163166:** WHERE clause of the form "x>=A".  Example:
        -:163167:**
        -:163168:**      x>A OR (x=A AND y>B)    adds:    x>=A
        -:163169:**
        -:163170:** The added conjunct can sometimes be helpful in query planning.
        -:163171:**
        -:163172:** CASE 3:
        -:163173:**
        -:163174:** If all subterms are indexable by a single table T, then set
        -:163175:**
        -:163176:**     WhereTerm.eOperator              =  WO_OR
        -:163177:**     WhereTerm.u.pOrInfo->indexable  |=  the cursor number for table T
        -:163178:**
        -:163179:** A subterm is "indexable" if it is of the form
        -:163180:** "T.C <op> <expr>" where C is any column of table T and
        -:163181:** <op> is one of "=", "<", "<=", ">", ">=", "IS NULL", or "IN".
        -:163182:** A subterm is also indexable if it is an AND of two or more
        -:163183:** subsubterms at least one of which is indexable.  Indexable AND
        -:163184:** subterms have their eOperator set to WO_AND and they have
        -:163185:** u.pAndInfo set to a dynamically allocated WhereAndTerm object.
        -:163186:**
        -:163187:** From another point of view, "indexable" means that the subterm could
        -:163188:** potentially be used with an index if an appropriate index exists.
        -:163189:** This analysis does not consider whether or not the index exists; that
        -:163190:** is decided elsewhere.  This analysis only looks at whether subterms
        -:163191:** appropriate for indexing exist.
        -:163192:**
        -:163193:** All examples A through E above satisfy case 3.  But if a term
        -:163194:** also satisfies case 1 (such as B) we know that the optimizer will
        -:163195:** always prefer case 1, so in that case we pretend that case 3 is not
        -:163196:** satisfied.
        -:163197:**
        -:163198:** It might be the case that multiple tables are indexable.  For example,
        -:163199:** (E) above is indexable on tables P, Q, and R.
        -:163200:**
        -:163201:** Terms that satisfy case 3 are candidates for lookup by using
        -:163202:** separate indices to find rowids for each subterm and composing
        -:163203:** the union of all rowids using a RowSet object.  This is similar
        -:163204:** to "bitmap indices" in other database engines.
        -:163205:**
        -:163206:** OTHERWISE:
        -:163207:**
        -:163208:** If none of cases 1, 2, or 3 apply, then leave the eOperator set to
        -:163209:** zero.  This term is not useful for search.
        -:163210:*/
