        -:169377:/* Implementation of the order-by-subquery optimization:
        -:169378:**
        -:169379:** WhereLoop pLoop, which the iLoop-th term of the nested loop, is really
        -:169380:** a subquery or CTE that has an ORDER BY clause.  See if any of the terms
        -:169381:** in the subquery ORDER BY clause will satisfy pOrderBy from the outer
        -:169382:** query.  Mark off all satisfied terms (by setting bits in *pOBSat) and
        -:169383:** return TRUE if they do.  If not, return false.
        -:169384:**
        -:169385:** Example:
        -:169386:**
        -:169387:**    CREATE TABLE t1(a,b,c, PRIMARY KEY(a,b));
        -:169388:**    CREATE TABLE t2(x,y);
        -:169389:**    WITH t3(p,q) AS MATERIALIZED (SELECT x+y, x-y FROM t2 ORDER BY x+y)
        -:169390:**       SELECT * FROM t3 JOIN t1 ON a=q ORDER BY p, b;
        -:169391:**
        -:169392:** The CTE named "t3" comes out in the natural order of "p", so the first
        -:169393:** first them of "ORDER BY p,b" is satisfied by a sequential scan of "t3"
        -:169394:** and sorting only needs to occur on the second term "b".
        -:169395:**
        -:169396:** Limitations:
        -:169397:**
        -:169398:** (1)  The optimization is not applied if the outer ORDER BY contains
        -:169399:**      a COLLATE clause.  The optimization might be applied if the
        -:169400:**      outer ORDER BY uses NULLS FIRST, NULLS LAST, ASC, and/or DESC as
        -:169401:**      long as the subquery ORDER BY does the same.  But if the
        -:169402:**      outer ORDER BY uses COLLATE, even a redundant COLLATE, the
        -:169403:**      optimization is bypassed.
        -:169404:**
        -:169405:** (2)  The subquery ORDER BY terms must exactly match subquery result
        -:169406:**      columns, including any COLLATE annotations.  This routine relies
        -:169407:**      on iOrderByCol to do matching between order by terms and result
        -:169408:**      columns, and iOrderByCol will not be set if the result column
        -:169409:**      and ORDER BY collations differ.
        -:169410:**
        -:169411:** (3)  The subquery and outer ORDER BY can be in opposite directions as
        -:169412:**      long as  the subquery is materialized.  If the subquery is
        -:169413:**      implemented as a co-routine, the sort orders must be in the same
        -:169414:**      direction because there is no way to run a co-routine backwards.
        -:169415:*/
