        -:171042:/*
        -:171043:** Generate the beginning of the loop used for WHERE clause processing.
        -:171044:** The return value is a pointer to an opaque structure that contains
        -:171045:** information needed to terminate the loop.  Later, the calling routine
        -:171046:** should invoke sqlite3WhereEnd() with the return value of this function
        -:171047:** in order to complete the WHERE clause processing.
        -:171048:**
        -:171049:** If an error occurs, this routine returns NULL.
        -:171050:**
        -:171051:** The basic idea is to do a nested loop, one loop for each table in
        -:171052:** the FROM clause of a select.  (INSERT and UPDATE statements are the
        -:171053:** same as a SELECT with only a single table in the FROM clause.)  For
        -:171054:** example, if the SQL is this:
        -:171055:**
        -:171056:**       SELECT * FROM t1, t2, t3 WHERE ...;
        -:171057:**
        -:171058:** Then the code generated is conceptually like the following:
        -:171059:**
        -:171060:**      foreach row1 in t1 do       \    Code generated
        -:171061:**        foreach row2 in t2 do      |-- by sqlite3WhereBegin()
        -:171062:**          foreach row3 in t3 do   /
        -:171063:**            ...
        -:171064:**          end                     \    Code generated
        -:171065:**        end                        |-- by sqlite3WhereEnd()
        -:171066:**      end                         /
        -:171067:**
        -:171068:** Note that the loops might not be nested in the order in which they
        -:171069:** appear in the FROM clause if a different order is better able to make
        -:171070:** use of indices.  Note also that when the IN operator appears in
        -:171071:** the WHERE clause, it might result in additional nested loops for
        -:171072:** scanning through all values on the right-hand side of the IN.
        -:171073:**
        -:171074:** There are Btree cursors associated with each table.  t1 uses cursor
        -:171075:** number pTabList->a[0].iCursor.  t2 uses the cursor pTabList->a[1].iCursor.
        -:171076:** And so forth.  This routine generates code to open those VDBE cursors
        -:171077:** and sqlite3WhereEnd() generates the code to close them.
        -:171078:**
        -:171079:** The code that sqlite3WhereBegin() generates leaves the cursors named
        -:171080:** in pTabList pointing at their appropriate entries.  The [...] code
        -:171081:** can use OP_Column and OP_Rowid opcodes on these cursors to extract
        -:171082:** data from the various tables of the loop.
        -:171083:**
        -:171084:** If the WHERE clause is empty, the foreach loops must each scan their
        -:171085:** entire tables.  Thus a three-way join is an O(N^3) operation.  But if
        -:171086:** the tables have indices and there are terms in the WHERE clause that
        -:171087:** refer to those indices, a complete table scan can be avoided and the
        -:171088:** code will run much faster.  Most of the work of this routine is checking
        -:171089:** to see if there are indices that can be used to speed up the loop.
        -:171090:**
        -:171091:** Terms of the WHERE clause are also used to limit which rows actually
        -:171092:** make it to the "..." in the middle of the loop.  After each "foreach",
        -:171093:** terms of the WHERE clause that use only terms in that loop and outer
        -:171094:** loops are evaluated and if false a jump is made around all subsequent
        -:171095:** inner loops (or around the "..." if the test occurs within the inner-
        -:171096:** most loop)
        -:171097:**
        -:171098:** OUTER JOINS
        -:171099:**
        -:171100:** An outer join of tables t1 and t2 is conceptually coded as follows:
        -:171101:**
        -:171102:**    foreach row1 in t1 do
        -:171103:**      flag = 0
        -:171104:**      foreach row2 in t2 do
        -:171105:**        start:
        -:171106:**          ...
        -:171107:**          flag = 1
        -:171108:**      end
        -:171109:**      if flag==0 then
        -:171110:**        move the row2 cursor to a null row
        -:171111:**        goto start
        -:171112:**      fi
        -:171113:**    end
        -:171114:**
        -:171115:** ORDER BY CLAUSE PROCESSING
        -:171116:**
        -:171117:** pOrderBy is a pointer to the ORDER BY clause (or the GROUP BY clause
