        -:172170:
        -:172171:/*
        -:172172:** SELECT REWRITING
        -:172173:**
        -:172174:**   Any SELECT statement that contains one or more window functions in
        -:172175:**   either the select list or ORDER BY clause (the only two places window
        -:172176:**   functions may be used) is transformed by function sqlite3WindowRewrite()
        -:172177:**   in order to support window function processing. For example, with the
        -:172178:**   schema:
        -:172179:**
        -:172180:**     CREATE TABLE t1(a, b, c, d, e, f, g);
        -:172181:**
        -:172182:**   the statement:
        -:172183:**
        -:172184:**     SELECT a+1, max(b) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d) FROM t1 ORDER BY e;
        -:172185:**
        -:172186:**   is transformed to:
        -:172187:**
        -:172188:**     SELECT a+1, max(b) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d) FROM (
        -:172189:**         SELECT a, e, c, d, b FROM t1 ORDER BY c, d
        -:172190:**     ) ORDER BY e;
        -:172191:**
        -:172192:**   The flattening optimization is disabled when processing this transformed
        -:172193:**   SELECT statement. This allows the implementation of the window function
        -:172194:**   (in this case max()) to process rows sorted in order of (c, d), which
        -:172195:**   makes things easier for obvious reasons. More generally:
        -:172196:**
        -:172197:**     * FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING clauses are all moved to
        -:172198:**       the sub-query.
        -:172199:**
        -:172200:**     * ORDER BY, LIMIT and OFFSET remain part of the parent query.
        -:172201:**
        -:172202:**     * Terminals from each of the expression trees that make up the
        -:172203:**       select-list and ORDER BY expressions in the parent query are
        -:172204:**       selected by the sub-query. For the purposes of the transformation,
        -:172205:**       terminals are column references and aggregate functions.
        -:172206:**
        -:172207:**   If there is more than one window function in the SELECT that uses
        -:172208:**   the same window declaration (the OVER bit), then a single scan may
        -:172209:**   be used to process more than one window function. For example:
        -:172210:**
        -:172211:**     SELECT max(b) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d),
        -:172212:**            min(e) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d)
        -:172213:**     FROM t1;
        -:172214:**
        -:172215:**   is transformed in the same way as the example above. However:
        -:172216:**
        -:172217:**     SELECT max(b) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d),
        -:172218:**            min(e) OVER (PARTITION BY a ORDER BY b)
        -:172219:**     FROM t1;
        -:172220:**
        -:172221:**   Must be transformed to:
        -:172222:**
        -:172223:**     SELECT max(b) OVER (PARTITION BY c ORDER BY d) FROM (
        -:172224:**         SELECT e, min(e) OVER (PARTITION BY a ORDER BY b), c, d, b FROM
        -:172225:**           SELECT a, e, c, d, b FROM t1 ORDER BY a, b
        -:172226:**         ) ORDER BY c, d
        -:172227:**     ) ORDER BY e;
        -:172228:**
        -:172229:**   so that both min() and max() may process rows in the order defined by
        -:172230:**   their respective window declarations.
        -:172231:**
        -:172232:** INTERFACE WITH SELECT.C
        -:172233:**
        -:172234:**   When processing the rewritten SELECT statement, code in select.c calls
        -:172235:**   sqlite3WhereBegin() to begin iterating through the results of the
        -:172236:**   sub-query, which is always implemented as a co-routine. It then calls
        -:172237:**   sqlite3WindowCodeStep() to process rows and finish the scan by calling
        -:172238:**   sqlite3WhereEnd().
        -:172239:**
        -:172240:**   sqlite3WindowCodeStep() generates VM code so that, for each row returned
        -:172241:**   by the sub-query a sub-routine (OP_Gosub) coded by select.c is invoked.
        -:172242:**   When the sub-routine is invoked:
        -:172243:**
        -:172244:**     * The results of all window-functions for the row are stored
        -:172245:**       in the associated Window.regResult registers.
        -:172246:**
        -:172247:**     * The required terminal values are stored in the current row of
        -:172248:**       temp table Window.iEphCsr.
        -:172249:**
        -:172250:**   In some cases, depending on the window frame and the specific window
        -:172251:**   functions invoked, sqlite3WindowCodeStep() caches each entire partition
        -:172252:**   in a temp table before returning any rows. In other cases it does not.
        -:172253:**   This detail is encapsulated within this file, the code generated by
        -:172254:**   select.c is the same in either case.
        -:172255:**
        -:172256:** BUILT-IN WINDOW FUNCTIONS
        -:172257:**
        -:172258:**   This implementation features the following built-in window functions:
        -:172259:**
        -:172260:**     row_number()
        -:172261:**     rank()
        -:172262:**     dense_rank()
        -:172263:**     percent_rank()
        -:172264:**     cume_dist()
        -:172265:**     ntile(N)
        -:172266:**     lead(expr [, offset [, default]])
        -:172267:**     lag(expr [, offset [, default]])
        -:172268:**     first_value(expr)
        -:172269:**     last_value(expr)
        -:172270:**     nth_value(expr, N)
        -:172271:**
        -:172272:**   These are the same built-in window functions supported by Postgres.
        -:172273:**   Although the behaviour of aggregate window functions (functions that
        -:172274:**   can be used as either aggregates or window functions) allows them to
        -:172275:**   be implemented using an API, built-in window functions are much more
        -:172276:**   esoteric. Additionally, some window functions (e.g. nth_value())
        -:172277:**   may only be implemented by caching the entire partition in memory.
        -:172278:**   As such, some built-in window functions use the same API as aggregate
        -:172279:**   window functions and some are implemented directly using VDBE
        -:172280:**   instructions. Additionally, for those functions that use the API, the
        -:172281:**   window frame is sometimes modified before the SELECT statement is
        -:172282:**   rewritten. For example, regardless of the specified window frame, the
        -:172283:**   row_number() function always uses:
        -:172284:**
        -:172285:**     ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
        -:172286:**
        -:172287:**   See sqlite3WindowUpdate() for details.
        -:172288:**
        -:172289:**   As well as some of the built-in window functions, aggregate window
        -:172290:**   functions min() and max() are implemented using VDBE instructions if
        -:172291:**   the start of the window frame is declared as anything other than
        -:172292:**   UNBOUNDED PRECEDING.
        -:172293:*/
        -:172294:
