        -:188133:**
        -:188134:**     * The FTS3 module is being built into the core of
        -:188135:**       SQLite (in which case SQLITE_ENABLE_FTS3 is defined).
        -:188136:*/
        -:188137:
        -:188138:/* The full-text index is stored in a series of b+tree (-like)
        -:188139:** structures called segments which map terms to doclists.  The
        -:188140:** structures are like b+trees in layout, but are constructed from the
        -:188141:** bottom up in optimal fashion and are not updatable.  Since trees
        -:188142:** are built from the bottom up, things will be described from the
        -:188143:** bottom up.
        -:188144:**
        -:188145:**
        -:188146:**** Varints ****
        -:188147:** The basic unit of encoding is a variable-length integer called a
        -:188148:** varint.  We encode variable-length integers in little-endian order
        -:188149:** using seven bits * per byte as follows:
        -:188150:**
        -:188151:** KEY:
        -:188152:**         A = 0xxxxxxx    7 bits of data and one flag bit
        -:188153:**         B = 1xxxxxxx    7 bits of data and one flag bit
        -:188154:**
        -:188155:**  7 bits - A
        -:188156:** 14 bits - BA
        -:188157:** 21 bits - BBA
        -:188158:** and so on.
        -:188159:**
        -:188160:** This is similar in concept to how sqlite encodes "varints" but
        -:188161:** the encoding is not the same.  SQLite varints are big-endian
        -:188162:** are are limited to 9 bytes in length whereas FTS3 varints are
        -:188163:** little-endian and can be up to 10 bytes in length (in theory).
        -:188164:**
        -:188165:** Example encodings:
        -:188166:**
        -:188167:**     1:    0x01
        -:188168:**   127:    0x7f
        -:188169:**   128:    0x81 0x00
        -:188170:**
        -:188171:**
        -:188172:**** Document lists ****
        -:188173:** A doclist (document list) holds a docid-sorted list of hits for a
        -:188174:** given term.  Doclists hold docids and associated token positions.
        -:188175:** A docid is the unique integer identifier for a single document.
        -:188176:** A position is the index of a word within the document.  The first
        -:188177:** word of the document has a position of 0.
        -:188178:**
        -:188179:** FTS3 used to optionally store character offsets using a compile-time
        -:188180:** option.  But that functionality is no longer supported.
        -:188181:**
        -:188182:** A doclist is stored like this:
        -:188183:**
        -:188184:** array {
        -:188185:**   varint docid;          (delta from previous doclist)
        -:188186:**   array {                (position list for column 0)
        -:188187:**     varint position;     (2 more than the delta from previous position)
        -:188188:**   }
        -:188189:**   array {
        -:188190:**     varint POS_COLUMN;   (marks start of position list for new column)
        -:188191:**     varint column;       (index of new column)
        -:188192:**     array {
        -:188193:**       varint position;   (2 more than the delta from previous position)
        -:188194:**     }
        -:188195:**   }
        -:188196:**   varint POS_END;        (marks end of positions for this document.
        -:188197:** }
        -:188198:**
        -:188199:** Here, array { X } means zero or more occurrences of X, adjacent in
        -:188200:** memory.  A "position" is an index of a token in the token stream
        -:188201:** generated by the tokenizer. Note that POS_END and POS_COLUMN occur
        -:188202:** in the same logical place as the position element, and act as sentinels
        -:188203:** ending a position list array.  POS_END is 0.  POS_COLUMN is 1.
        -:188204:** The positions numbers are not stored literally but rather as two more
        -:188205:** than the difference from the prior position, or the just the position plus
        -:188206:** 2 for the first position.  Example:
        -:188207:**
        -:188208:**   label:       A B C D E  F  G H   I  J K
        -:188209:**   value:     123 5 9 1 1 14 35 0 234 72 0
        -:188210:**
        -:188211:** The 123 value is the first docid.  For column zero in this document
        -:188212:** there are two matches at positions 3 and 10 (5-2 and 9-2+3).  The 1
        -:188213:** at D signals the start of a new column; the 1 at E indicates that the
        -:188214:** new column is column number 1.  There are two positions at 12 and 45
        -:188215:** (14-2 and 35-2+12).  The 0 at H indicate the end-of-document.  The
        -:188216:** 234 at I is the delta to next docid (357).  It has one position 70
        -:188217:** (72-2) and then terminates with the 0 at K.
        -:188218:**
        -:188219:** A "position-list" is the list of positions for multiple columns for
        -:188220:** a single docid.  A "column-list" is the set of positions for a single
        -:188221:** column.  Hence, a position-list consists of one or more column-lists,
        -:188222:** a document record consists of a docid followed by a position-list and
        -:188223:** a doclist consists of one or more document records.
        -:188224:**
        -:188225:** A bare doclist omits the position information, becoming an
        -:188226:** array of varint-encoded docids.
        -:188227:**
        -:188228:**** Segment leaf nodes ****
        -:188229:** Segment leaf nodes store terms and doclists, ordered by term.  Leaf
        -:188230:** nodes are written using LeafWriter, and read using LeafReader (to
        -:188231:** iterate through a single leaf node's data) and LeavesReader (to
        -:188232:** iterate through a segment's entire leaf layer).  Leaf nodes have
        -:188233:** the format:
        -:188234:**
        -:188235:** varint iHeight;             (height from leaf level, always 0)
        -:188236:** varint nTerm;               (length of first term)
        -:188237:** char pTerm[nTerm];          (content of first term)
        -:188238:** varint nDoclist;            (length of term's associated doclist)
        -:188239:** char pDoclist[nDoclist];    (content of doclist)
        -:188240:** array {
        -:188241:**                             (further terms are delta-encoded)
        -:188242:**   varint nPrefix;           (length of prefix shared with previous term)
        -:188243:**   varint nSuffix;           (length of unshared suffix)
        -:188244:**   char pTermSuffix[nSuffix];(unshared suffix of next term)
        -:188245:**   varint nDoclist;          (length of term's associated doclist)
        -:188246:**   char pDoclist[nDoclist];  (content of doclist)
        -:188247:** }
        -:188248:**
        -:188249:** Here, array { X } means zero or more occurrences of X, adjacent in
        -:188250:** memory.
        -:188251:**
        -:188252:** Leaf nodes are broken into blocks which are stored contiguously in
        -:188253:** the %_segments table in sorted order.  This means that when the end
        -:188254:** of a node is reached, the next term is in the node with the next
        -:188255:** greater node id.
        -:188256:**
        -:188257:** New data is spilled to a new leaf node when the current node
        -:188258:** exceeds LEAF_MAX bytes (default 2048).  New data which itself is
        -:188259:** larger than STANDALONE_MIN (default 1024) is placed in a standalone
        -:188260:** node (a leaf node with a single term and doclist).  The goal of
        -:188261:** these settings is to pack together groups of small doclists while
        -:188262:** making it efficient to directly access large doclists.  The
        -:188263:** assumption is that large doclists represent terms which are more
        -:188264:** likely to be query targets.
        -:188265:**
        -:188266:** TODO(shess) It may be useful for blocking decisions to be more
        -:188267:** dynamic.  For instance, it may make more sense to have a 2.5k leaf
        -:188268:** node rather than splitting into 2k and .5k nodes.  My intuition is
        -:188269:** that this might extend through 2x or 4x the pagesize.
        -:188270:**
        -:188271:**
        -:188272:**** Segment interior nodes ****
        -:188273:** Segment interior nodes store blockids for subtree nodes and terms
        -:188274:** to describe what data is stored by the each subtree.  Interior
        -:188275:** nodes are written using InteriorWriter, and read using
        -:188276:** InteriorReader.  InteriorWriters are created as needed when
        -:188277:** SegmentWriter creates new leaf nodes, or when an interior node
        -:188278:** itself grows too big and must be split.  The format of interior
        -:188279:** nodes:
        -:188280:**
        -:188281:** varint iHeight;           (height from leaf level, always >0)
        -:188282:** varint iBlockid;          (block id of node's leftmost subtree)
        -:188283:** optional {
        -:188284:**   varint nTerm;           (length of first term)
        -:188285:**   char pTerm[nTerm];      (content of first term)
        -:188286:**   array {
        -:188287:**                                (further terms are delta-encoded)
        -:188288:**     varint nPrefix;            (length of shared prefix with previous term)
        -:188289:**     varint nSuffix;            (length of unshared suffix)
        -:188290:**     char pTermSuffix[nSuffix]; (unshared suffix of next term)
        -:188291:**   }
        -:188292:** }
        -:188293:**
        -:188294:** Here, optional { X } means an optional element, while array { X }
        -:188295:** means zero or more occurrences of X, adjacent in memory.
        -:188296:**
        -:188297:** An interior node encodes n terms separating n+1 subtrees.  The
        -:188298:** subtree blocks are contiguous, so only the first subtree's blockid
        -:188299:** is encoded.  The subtree at iBlockid will contain all terms less
        -:188300:** than the first term encoded (or all terms if no term is encoded).
        -:188301:** Otherwise, for terms greater than or equal to pTerm[i] but less
        -:188302:** than pTerm[i+1], the subtree for that term will be rooted at
        -:188303:** iBlockid+i.  Interior nodes only store enough term data to
        -:188304:** distinguish adjacent children (if the rightmost term of the left
        -:188305:** child is "something", and the leftmost term of the right child is
        -:188306:** "wicked", only "w" is stored).
        -:188307:**
        -:188308:** New data is spilled to a new interior node at the same height when
        -:188309:** the current node exceeds INTERIOR_MAX bytes (default 2048).
        -:188310:** INTERIOR_MIN_TERMS (default 7) keeps large terms from monopolizing
        -:188311:** interior nodes and making the tree too skinny.  The interior nodes
        -:188312:** at a given height are naturally tracked by interior nodes at
        -:188313:** height+1, and so on.
        -:188314:**
        -:188315:**
        -:188316:**** Segment directory ****
        -:188317:** The segment directory in table %_segdir stores meta-information for
        -:188318:** merging and deleting segments, and also the root node of the
        -:188319:** segment's tree.
        -:188320:**
        -:188321:** The root node is the top node of the segment's tree after encoding
        -:188322:** the entire segment, restricted to ROOT_MAX bytes (default 1024).
        -:188323:** This could be either a leaf node or an interior node.  If the top
        -:188324:** node requires more than ROOT_MAX bytes, it is flushed to %_segments
        -:188325:** and a new root interior node is generated (which should always fit
        -:188326:** within ROOT_MAX because it only needs space for 2 varints, the
        -:188327:** height and the blockid of the previous root).
        -:188328:**
        -:188329:** The meta-information in the segment directory is:
        -:188330:**   level               - segment level (see below)
        -:188331:**   idx                 - index within level
        -:188332:**                       - (level,idx uniquely identify a segment)
        -:188333:**   start_block         - first leaf node
        -:188334:**   leaves_end_block    - last leaf node
        -:188335:**   end_block           - last block (including interior nodes)
        -:188336:**   root                - contents of root node
        -:188337:**
        -:188338:** If the root node is a leaf node, then start_block,
        -:188339:** leaves_end_block, and end_block are all 0.
        -:188340:**
        -:188341:**
        -:188342:**** Segment merging ****
        -:188343:** To amortize update costs, segments are grouped into levels and
        -:188344:** merged in batches.  Each increase in level represents exponentially
        -:188345:** more documents.
        -:188346:**
        -:188347:** New documents (actually, document updates) are tokenized and
        -:188348:** written individually (using LeafWriter) to a level 0 segment, with
        -:188349:** incrementing idx.  When idx reaches MERGE_COUNT (default 16), all
        -:188350:** level 0 segments are merged into a single level 1 segment.  Level 1
        -:188351:** is populated like level 0, and eventually MERGE_COUNT level 1
        -:188352:** segments are merged to a single level 2 segment (representing
        -:188353:** MERGE_COUNT^2 updates), and so on.
        -:188354:**
        -:188355:** A segment merge traverses all segments at a given level in
        -:188356:** parallel, performing a straightforward sorted merge.  Since segment
        -:188357:** leaf nodes are written in to the %_segments table in order, this
        -:188358:** merge traverses the underlying sqlite disk structures efficiently.
        -:188359:** After the merge, all segment blocks from the merged level are
        -:188360:** deleted.
        -:188361:**
        -:188362:** MERGE_COUNT controls how often we merge segments.  16 seems to be
        -:188363:** somewhat of a sweet spot for insertion performance.  32 and 64 show
        -:188364:** very similar performance numbers to 16 on insertion, though they're
        -:188365:** a tiny bit slower (perhaps due to more overhead in merge-time
        -:188366:** sorting).  8 is about 20% slower than 16, 4 about 50% slower than
        -:188367:** 16, 2 about 66% slower than 16.
        -:188368:**
        -:188369:** At query time, high MERGE_COUNT increases the number of segments
        -:188370:** which need to be scanned and merged.  For instance, with 100k docs
        -:188371:** inserted:
        -:188372:**
        -:188373:**    MERGE_COUNT   segments
        -:188374:**       16           25
        -:188375:**        8           12
        -:188376:**        4           10
        -:188377:**        2            6
        -:188378:**
        -:188379:** This appears to have only a moderate impact on queries for very
        -:188380:** frequent terms (which are somewhat dominated by segment merge
        -:188381:** costs), and infrequent and non-existent terms still seem to be fast
        -:188382:** even with many segments.
        -:188383:**
        -:188384:** TODO(shess) That said, it would be nice to have a better query-side
        -:188385:** argument for MERGE_COUNT of 16.  Also, it is possible/likely that
        -:188386:** optimizations to things like doclist merging will swing the sweet
        -:188387:** spot around.
        -:188388:**
        -:188389:**
        -:188390:**
        -:188391:**** Handling of deletions and updates ****
        -:188392:** Since we're using a segmented structure, with no docid-oriented
        -:188393:** index into the term index, we clearly cannot simply update the term
        -:188394:** index when a document is deleted or updated.  For deletions, we
        -:188395:** write an empty doclist (varint(docid) varint(POS_END)), for updates
        -:188396:** we simply write the new doclist.  Segment merges overwrite older
        -:188397:** data for a particular docid with newer data, so deletes or updates
        -:188398:** will eventually overtake the earlier data and knock it out.  The
        -:188399:** query logic likewise merges doclists so that newer data knocks out
        -:188400:** older data.
        -:188401:*/
        -:188402:
        -:188403:/************** Include fts3Int.h in the middle of fts3.c ********************/
        -:188404:/************** Begin file fts3Int.h *****************************************/
        -:188405:/*
        -:188406:** 2009 Nov 12
        -:188407:**
        -:188408:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:188409:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:188410:**
