        -:198452:/*
        -:198453:** Implementation of a special SQL scalar function for testing tokenizers
        -:198454:** designed to be used in concert with the Tcl testing framework. This
        -:198455:** function must be called with two or more arguments:
        -:198456:**
        -:198457:**   SELECT <function-name>(<key-name>, ..., <input-string>);
        -:198458:**
        -:198459:** where <function-name> is the name passed as the second argument
        -:198460:** to the sqlite3Fts3InitHashTable() function (e.g. 'fts3_tokenizer')
        -:198461:** concatenated with the string '_test' (e.g. 'fts3_tokenizer_test').
        -:198462:**
        -:198463:** The return value is a string that may be interpreted as a Tcl
        -:198464:** list. For each token in the <input-string>, three elements are
        -:198465:** added to the returned list. The first is the token position, the
        -:198466:** second is the token text (folded, stemmed, etc.) and the third is the
        -:198467:** substring of <input-string> associated with the token. For example,
        -:198468:** using the built-in "simple" tokenizer:
        -:198469:**
        -:198470:**   SELECT fts_tokenizer_test('simple', 'I don't see how');
        -:198471:**
        -:198472:** will return the string:
        -:198473:**
        -:198474:**   "{0 i I 1 dont don't 2 see see 3 how how}"
        -:198475:**
        -:198476:*/
        -:198477:static void testFunc(
        -:198478:  sqlite3_context *context,
        -:198479:  int argc,
        -:198480:  sqlite3_value **argv
        -:198481:){
        -:198482:  Fts3Hash *pHash;
