        -:199467:
        -:199468:/*
        -:199469:** When full-text index nodes are loaded from disk, the buffer that they
        -:199470:** are loaded into has the following number of bytes of padding at the end
        -:199471:** of it. i.e. if a full-text index node is 900 bytes in size, then a buffer
        -:199472:** of 920 bytes is allocated for it.
        -:199473:**
        -:199474:** This means that if we have a pointer into a buffer containing node data,
        -:199475:** it is always safe to read up to two varints from it without risking an
        -:199476:** overread, even if the node data is corrupted.
        -:199477:*/
        -:199478:#define FTS3_NODE_PADDING (FTS3_VARINT_MAX*2)
        -:199479:
        -:199480:/*
        -:199481:** Under certain circumstances, b-tree nodes (doclists) can be loaded into
        -:199482:** memory incrementally instead of all at once. This can be a big performance
        -:199483:** win (reduced IO and CPU) if SQLite stops calling the virtual table xNext()
        -:199484:** method before retrieving all query results (as may happen, for example,
        -:199485:** if a query has a LIMIT clause).
        -:199486:**
        -:199487:** Incremental loading is used for b-tree nodes FTS3_NODE_CHUNK_THRESHOLD
        -:199488:** bytes and larger. Nodes are loaded in chunks of FTS3_NODE_CHUNKSIZE bytes.
        -:199489:** The code is written so that the hard lower-limit for each of these values
        -:199490:** is 1. Clearly such small values would be inefficient, but can be useful
        -:199491:** for testing purposes.
        -:199492:**
        -:199493:** If this module is built with SQLITE_TEST defined, these constants may
        -:199494:** be overridden at runtime for testing purposes. File fts3_test.c contains
        -:199495:** a Tcl interface to read and write the values.
        -:199496:*/
        -:199497:#ifdef SQLITE_TEST
        -:199498:int test_fts3_node_chunksize = (4*1024);
        -:199499:int test_fts3_node_chunk_threshold = (4*1024)*4;
        -:199500:# define FTS3_NODE_CHUNKSIZE       test_fts3_node_chunksize
        -:199501:# define FTS3_NODE_CHUNK_THRESHOLD test_fts3_node_chunk_threshold
        -:199502:#else
        -:199503:# define FTS3_NODE_CHUNKSIZE (4*1024)
        -:199504:# define FTS3_NODE_CHUNK_THRESHOLD (FTS3_NODE_CHUNKSIZE*4)
        -:199505:#endif
        -:199506:
        -:199507:/*
        -:199508:** The values that may be meaningfully bound to the :1 parameter in
        -:199509:** statements SQL_REPLACE_STAT and SQL_SELECT_STAT.
