        -:207847:/*
        -:207848:** 2015-08-12
        -:207849:**
        -:207850:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:207851:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:207852:**
        -:207853:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:207854:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:207855:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:207856:**
        -:207857:******************************************************************************
        -:207858:**
        -:207859:** SQLite JSON functions.
        -:207860:**
        -:207861:** This file began as an extension in ext/misc/json1.c in 2015.  That
        -:207862:** extension proved so useful that it has now been moved into the core.
        -:207863:**
        -:207864:** The original design stored all JSON as pure text, canonical RFC-8259.
        -:207865:** Support for JSON-5 extensions was added with version 3.42.0 (2023-05-16).
        -:207866:** All generated JSON text still conforms strictly to RFC-8259, but text
        -:207867:** with JSON-5 extensions is accepted as input.
        -:207868:**
        -:207869:** Beginning with version 3.45.0 (circa 2024-01-01), these routines also
        -:207870:** accept BLOB values that have JSON encoded using a binary representation
        -:207871:** called "JSONB".  The name JSONB comes from PostgreSQL, however the on-disk
        -:207872:** format for SQLite-JSONB is completely different and incompatible with
        -:207873:** PostgreSQL-JSONB.
        -:207874:**
        -:207875:** Decoding and interpreting JSONB is still O(N) where N is the size of
        -:207876:** the input, the same as text JSON.  However, the constant of proportionality
        -:207877:** for JSONB is much smaller due to faster parsing.  The size of each
        -:207878:** element in JSONB is encoded in its header, so there is no need to search
        -:207879:** for delimiters using persnickety syntax rules.  JSONB seems to be about
        -:207880:** 3x faster than text JSON as a result.  JSONB is also tends to be slightly
        -:207881:** smaller than text JSON, by 5% or 10%, but there are corner cases where
        -:207882:** JSONB can be slightly larger.  So you are not far mistaken to say that
        -:207883:** a JSONB blob is the same size as the equivalent RFC-8259 text.
        -:207884:**
        -:207885:**
        -:207886:** THE JSONB ENCODING:
        -:207887:**
        -:207888:** Every JSON element is encoded in JSONB as a header and a payload.
        -:207889:** The header is between 1 and 9 bytes in size.  The payload is zero
        -:207890:** or more bytes.
        -:207891:**
        -:207892:** The lower 4 bits of the first byte of the header determines the
        -:207893:** element type:
        -:207894:**
        -:207895:**    0:   NULL
        -:207896:**    1:   TRUE
        -:207897:**    2:   FALSE
        -:207898:**    3:   INT        -- RFC-8259 integer literal
        -:207899:**    4:   INT5       -- JSON5 integer literal
        -:207900:**    5:   FLOAT      -- RFC-8259 floating point literal
        -:207901:**    6:   FLOAT5     -- JSON5 floating point literal
        -:207902:**    7:   TEXT       -- Text literal acceptable to both SQL and JSON
        -:207903:**    8:   TEXTJ      -- Text containing RFC-8259 escapes
        -:207904:**    9:   TEXT5      -- Text containing JSON5 and/or RFC-8259 escapes
        -:207905:**   10:   TEXTRAW    -- Text containing unescaped syntax characters
        -:207906:**   11:   ARRAY
        -:207907:**   12:   OBJECT
        -:207908:**
        -:207909:** The other three possible values (13-15) are reserved for future
        -:207910:** enhancements.
        -:207911:**
        -:207912:** The upper 4 bits of the first byte determine the size of the header
        -:207913:** and sometimes also the size of the payload.  If X is the first byte
        -:207914:** of the element and if X>>4 is between 0 and 11, then the payload
        -:207915:** will be that many bytes in size and the header is exactly one byte
        -:207916:** in size.  Other four values for X>>4 (12-15) indicate that the header
        -:207917:** is more than one byte in size and that the payload size is determined
        -:207918:** by the remainder of the header, interpreted as a unsigned big-endian
        -:207919:** integer.
        -:207920:**
        -:207921:**   Value of X>>4         Size integer        Total header size
        -:207922:**   -------------     --------------------    -----------------
        -:207923:**        12           1 byte (0-255)                2
        -:207924:**        13           2 byte (0-65535)              3
        -:207925:**        14           4 byte (0-4294967295)         5
        -:207926:**        15           8 byte (0-1.8e19)             9
        -:207927:**
        -:207928:** The payload size need not be expressed in its minimal form.  For example,
        -:207929:** if the payload size is 10, the size can be expressed in any of 5 different
        -:207930:** ways: (1) (X>>4)==10, (2) (X>>4)==12 following by one 0x0a byte,
        -:207931:** (3) (X>>4)==13 followed by 0x00 and 0x0a, (4) (X>>4)==14 followed by
        -:207932:** 0x00 0x00 0x00 0x0a, or (5) (X>>4)==15 followed by 7 bytes of 0x00 and
        -:207933:** a single byte of 0x0a.  The shorter forms are preferred, of course, but
        -:207934:** sometimes when generating JSONB, the payload size is not known in advance
        -:207935:** and it is convenient to reserve sufficient header space to cover the
        -:207936:** largest possible payload size and then come back later and patch up
        -:207937:** the size when it becomes known, resulting in a non-minimal encoding.
        -:207938:**
        -:207939:** The value (X>>4)==15 is not actually used in the current implementation
        -:207940:** (as SQLite is currently unable to handle BLOBs larger than about 2GB)
        -:207941:** but is included in the design to allow for future enhancements.
        -:207942:**
        -:207943:** The payload follows the header.  NULL, TRUE, and FALSE have no payload and
        -:207944:** their payload size must always be zero.  The payload for INT, INT5,
        -:207945:** FLOAT, FLOAT5, TEXT, TEXTJ, TEXT5, and TEXTROW is text.  Note that the
        -:207946:** "..." or '...' delimiters are omitted from the various text encodings.
        -:207947:** The payload for ARRAY and OBJECT is a list of additional elements that
        -:207948:** are the content for the array or object.  The payload for an OBJECT
        -:207949:** must be an even number of elements.  The first element of each pair is
        -:207950:** the label and must be of type TEXT, TEXTJ, TEXT5, or TEXTRAW.
        -:207951:**
        -:207952:** A valid JSONB blob consists of a single element, as described above.
        -:207953:** Usually this will be an ARRAY or OBJECT element which has many more
        -:207954:** elements as its content.  But the overall blob is just a single element.
        -:207955:**
        -:207956:** Input validation for JSONB blobs simply checks that the element type
        -:207957:** code is between 0 and 12 and that the total size of the element
        -:207958:** (header plus payload) is the same as the size of the BLOB.  If those
        -:207959:** checks are true, the BLOB is assumed to be JSONB and processing continues.
        -:207960:** Errors are only raised if some other miscoding is discovered during
        -:207961:** processing.
        -:207962:**
        -:207963:** Additional information can be found in the doc/jsonb.md file of the
        -:207964:** canonical SQLite source tree.
        -:207965:*/
        -:207966:#ifndef SQLITE_OMIT_JSON
