        -:220636:/************** Begin file sqlite3rbu.c **************************************/
        -:220637:/*
        -:220638:** 2014 August 30
        -:220639:**
        -:220640:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:220641:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:220642:**
        -:220643:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:220644:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:220645:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:220646:**
        -:220647:*************************************************************************
        -:220648:**
        -:220649:**
        -:220650:** OVERVIEW
        -:220651:**
        -:220652:**  The RBU extension requires that the RBU update be packaged as an
        -:220653:**  SQLite database. The tables it expects to find are described in
        -:220654:**  sqlite3rbu.h.  Essentially, for each table xyz in the target database
        -:220655:**  that the user wishes to write to, a corresponding data_xyz table is
        -:220656:**  created in the RBU database and populated with one row for each row to
        -:220657:**  update, insert or delete from the target table.
        -:220658:**
        -:220659:**  The update proceeds in three stages:
        -:220660:**
        -:220661:**  1) The database is updated. The modified database pages are written
        -:220662:**     to a *-oal file. A *-oal file is just like a *-wal file, except
        -:220663:**     that it is named "<database>-oal" instead of "<database>-wal".
        -:220664:**     Because regular SQLite clients do not look for file named
        -:220665:**     "<database>-oal", they go on using the original database in
        -:220666:**     rollback mode while the *-oal file is being generated.
        -:220667:**
        -:220668:**     During this stage RBU does not update the database by writing
        -:220669:**     directly to the target tables. Instead it creates "imposter"
        -:220670:**     tables using the SQLITE_TESTCTRL_IMPOSTER interface that it uses
        -:220671:**     to update each b-tree individually. All updates required by each
        -:220672:**     b-tree are completed before moving on to the next, and all
        -:220673:**     updates are done in sorted key order.
        -:220674:**
        -:220675:**  2) The "<database>-oal" file is moved to the equivalent "<database>-wal"
        -:220676:**     location using a call to rename(2). Before doing this the RBU
        -:220677:**     module takes an EXCLUSIVE lock on the database file, ensuring
        -:220678:**     that there are no other active readers.
        -:220679:**
        -:220680:**     Once the EXCLUSIVE lock is released, any other database readers
        -:220681:**     detect the new *-wal file and read the database in wal mode. At
        -:220682:**     this point they see the new version of the database - including
        -:220683:**     the updates made as part of the RBU update.
        -:220684:**
        -:220685:**  3) The new *-wal file is checkpointed. This proceeds in the same way
        -:220686:**     as a regular database checkpoint, except that a single frame is
        -:220687:**     checkpointed each time sqlite3rbu_step() is called. If the RBU
        -:220688:**     handle is closed before the entire *-wal file is checkpointed,
        -:220689:**     the checkpoint progress is saved in the RBU database and the
        -:220690:**     checkpoint can be resumed by another RBU client at some point in
        -:220691:**     the future.
        -:220692:**
        -:220693:** POTENTIAL PROBLEMS
        -:220694:**
        -:220695:**  The rename() call might not be portable. And RBU is not currently
        -:220696:**  syncing the directory after renaming the file.
        -:220697:**
        -:220698:**  When state is saved, any commit to the *-oal file and the commit to
        -:220699:**  the RBU update database are not atomic. So if the power fails at the
        -:220700:**  wrong moment they might get out of sync. As the main database will be
        -:220701:**  committed before the RBU update database this will likely either just
        -:220702:**  pass unnoticed, or result in SQLITE_CONSTRAINT errors (due to UNIQUE
        -:220703:**  constraint violations).
        -:220704:**
        -:220705:**  If some client does modify the target database mid RBU update, or some
        -:220706:**  other error occurs, the RBU extension will keep throwing errors. It's
        -:220707:**  not really clear how to get out of this state. The system could just
        -:220708:**  by delete the RBU update database and *-oal file and have the device
        -:220709:**  download the update again and start over.
        -:220710:**
        -:220711:**  At present, for an UPDATE, both the new.* and old.* records are
        -:220712:**  collected in the rbu_xyz table. And for both UPDATEs and DELETEs all
        -:220713:**  fields are collected.  This means we're probably writing a lot more
        -:220714:**  data to disk when saving the state of an ongoing update to the RBU
        -:220715:**  update database than is strictly necessary.
        -:220716:**
        -:220717:*/
        -:220718:
        -:220719:/* #include <assert.h> */
        -:220720:/* #include <string.h> */
        -:220721:/* #include <stdio.h> */
        -:220722:
        -:220723:/* #include "sqlite3.h" */
        -:220724:
        -:220725:#if !defined(SQLITE_CORE) || defined(SQLITE_ENABLE_RBU)
        -:220726:/************** Include sqlite3rbu.h in the middle of sqlite3rbu.c ***********/
        -:220727:/************** Begin file sqlite3rbu.h **************************************/
        -:220728:/*
        -:220729:** 2014 August 30
        -:220730:**
        -:220731:** The author disclaims copyright to this source code.  In place of
        -:220732:** a legal notice, here is a blessing:
        -:220733:**
        -:220734:**    May you do good and not evil.
        -:220735:**    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
        -:220736:**    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.
        -:220737:**
        -:220738:*************************************************************************
        -:220739:**
        -:220740:** This file contains the public interface for the RBU extension.
        -:220741:*/
        -:220742:
        -:220743:/*
        -:220744:** SUMMARY
        -:220745:**
        -:220746:** Writing a transaction containing a large number of operations on
        -:220747:** b-tree indexes that are collectively larger than the available cache
        -:220748:** memory can be very inefficient.
        -:220749:**
        -:220750:** The problem is that in order to update a b-tree, the leaf page (at least)
        -:220751:** containing the entry being inserted or deleted must be modified. If the
        -:220752:** working set of leaves is larger than the available cache memory, then a
        -:220753:** single leaf that is modified more than once as part of the transaction
        -:220754:** may be loaded from or written to the persistent media multiple times.
        -:220755:** Additionally, because the index updates are likely to be applied in
        -:220756:** random order, access to pages within the database is also likely to be in
        -:220757:** random order, which is itself quite inefficient.
        -:220758:**
        -:220759:** One way to improve the situation is to sort the operations on each index
        -:220760:** by index key before applying them to the b-tree. This leads to an IO
        -:220761:** pattern that resembles a single linear scan through the index b-tree,
        -:220762:** and all but guarantees each modified leaf page is loaded and stored
        -:220763:** exactly once. SQLite uses this trick to improve the performance of
        -:220764:** CREATE INDEX commands. This extension allows it to be used to improve
        -:220765:** the performance of large transactions on existing databases.
        -:220766:**
        -:220767:** Additionally, this extension allows the work involved in writing the
        -:220768:** large transaction to be broken down into sub-transactions performed
        -:220769:** sequentially by separate processes. This is useful if the system cannot
        -:220770:** guarantee that a single update process will run for long enough to apply
        -:220771:** the entire update, for example because the update is being applied on a
        -:220772:** mobile device that is frequently rebooted. Even after the writer process
        -:220773:** has committed one or more sub-transactions, other database clients continue
        -:220774:** to read from the original database snapshot. In other words, partially
        -:220775:** applied transactions are not visible to other clients.
        -:220776:**
        -:220777:** "RBU" stands for "Resumable Bulk Update". As in a large database update
        -:220778:** transmitted via a wireless network to a mobile device. A transaction
        -:220779:** applied using this extension is hence referred to as an "RBU update".
        -:220780:**
        -:220781:**
        -:220782:** LIMITATIONS
        -:220783:**
        -:220784:** An "RBU update" transaction is subject to the following limitations:
        -:220785:**
        -:220786:**   * The transaction must consist of INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE operations
        -:220787:**     only.
        -:220788:**
        -:220789:**   * INSERT statements may not use any default values.
        -:220790:**
        -:220791:**   * UPDATE and DELETE statements must identify their target rows by
        -:220792:**     non-NULL PRIMARY KEY values. Rows with NULL values stored in PRIMARY
        -:220793:**     KEY fields may not be updated or deleted. If the table being written
        -:220794:**     has no PRIMARY KEY, affected rows must be identified by rowid.
        -:220795:**
        -:220796:**   * UPDATE statements may not modify PRIMARY KEY columns.
        -:220797:**
        -:220798:**   * No triggers will be fired.
        -:220799:**
        -:220800:**   * No foreign key violations are detected or reported.
        -:220801:**
        -:220802:**   * CHECK constraints are not enforced.
        -:220803:**
        -:220804:**   * No constraint handling mode except for "OR ROLLBACK" is supported.
        -:220805:**
        -:220806:**
        -:220807:** PREPARATION
        -:220808:**
        -:220809:** An "RBU update" is stored as a separate SQLite database. A database
        -:220810:** containing an RBU update is an "RBU database". For each table in the
        -:220811:** target database to be updated, the RBU database should contain a table
        -:220812:** named "data_<target name>" containing the same set of columns as the
        -:220813:** target table, and one more - "rbu_control". The data_% table should
        -:220814:** have no PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE constraints, but each column should have
        -:220815:** the same type as the corresponding column in the target database.
        -:220816:** The "rbu_control" column should have no type at all. For example, if
        -:220817:** the target database contains:
        -:220818:**
        -:220819:**   CREATE TABLE t1(a INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, b TEXT, c UNIQUE);
        -:220820:**
        -:220821:** Then the RBU database should contain:
        -:220822:**
        -:220823:**   CREATE TABLE data_t1(a INTEGER, b TEXT, c, rbu_control);
        -:220824:**
        -:220825:** The order of the columns in the data_% table does not matter.
        -:220826:**
        -:220827:** Instead of a regular table, the RBU database may also contain virtual
        -:220828:** tables or views named using the data_<target> naming scheme.
        -:220829:**
        -:220830:** Instead of the plain data_<target> naming scheme, RBU database tables
        -:220831:** may also be named data<integer>_<target>, where <integer> is any sequence
        -:220832:** of zero or more numeric characters (0-9). This can be significant because
        -:220833:** tables within the RBU database are always processed in order sorted by
        -:220834:** name. By judicious selection of the <integer> portion of the names
        -:220835:** of the RBU tables the user can therefore control the order in which they
        -:220836:** are processed. This can be useful, for example, to ensure that "external
        -:220837:** content" FTS4 tables are updated before their underlying content tables.
        -:220838:**
        -:220839:** If the target database table is a virtual table or a table that has no
        -:220840:** PRIMARY KEY declaration, the data_% table must also contain a column
        -:220841:** named "rbu_rowid". This column is mapped to the table's implicit primary
        -:220842:** key column - "rowid". Virtual tables for which the "rowid" column does
        -:220843:** not function like a primary key value cannot be updated using RBU. For
        -:220844:** example, if the target db contains either of the following:
        -:220845:**
        -:220846:**   CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE x1 USING fts3(a, b);
        -:220847:**   CREATE TABLE x1(a, b)
        -:220848:**
        -:220849:** then the RBU database should contain:
        -:220850:**
        -:220851:**   CREATE TABLE data_x1(a, b, rbu_rowid, rbu_control);
        -:220852:**
        -:220853:** All non-hidden columns (i.e. all columns matched by "SELECT *") of the
        -:220854:** target table must be present in the input table. For virtual tables,
        -:220855:** hidden columns are optional - they are updated by RBU if present in
        -:220856:** the input table, or not otherwise. For example, to write to an fts4
        -:220857:** table with a hidden languageid column such as:
        -:220858:**
        -:220859:**   CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE ft1 USING fts4(a, b, languageid='langid');
        -:220860:**
        -:220861:** Either of the following input table schemas may be used:
        -:220862:**
        -:220863:**   CREATE TABLE data_ft1(a, b, langid, rbu_rowid, rbu_control);
        -:220864:**   CREATE TABLE data_ft1(a, b, rbu_rowid, rbu_control);
        -:220865:**
        -:220866:** For each row to INSERT into the target database as part of the RBU
        -:220867:** update, the corresponding data_% table should contain a single record
        -:220868:** with the "rbu_control" column set to contain integer value 0. The
        -:220869:** other columns should be set to the values that make up the new record
        -:220870:** to insert.
        -:220871:**
        -:220872:** If the target database table has an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, it is not
        -:220873:** possible to insert a NULL value into the IPK column. Attempting to
        -:220874:** do so results in an SQLITE_MISMATCH error.
        -:220875:**
        -:220876:** For each row to DELETE from the target database as part of the RBU
        -:220877:** update, the corresponding data_% table should contain a single record
        -:220878:** with the "rbu_control" column set to contain integer value 1. The
        -:220879:** real primary key values of the row to delete should be stored in the
        -:220880:** corresponding columns of the data_% table. The values stored in the
        -:220881:** other columns are not used.
        -:220882:**
        -:220883:** For each row to UPDATE from the target database as part of the RBU
        -:220884:** update, the corresponding data_% table should contain a single record
        -:220885:** with the "rbu_control" column set to contain a value of type text.
        -:220886:** The real primary key values identifying the row to update should be
        -:220887:** stored in the corresponding columns of the data_% table row, as should
        -:220888:** the new values of all columns being update. The text value in the
        -:220889:** "rbu_control" column must contain the same number of characters as
        -:220890:** there are columns in the target database table, and must consist entirely
        -:220891:** of 'x' and '.' characters (or in some special cases 'd' - see below). For
        -:220892:** each column that is being updated, the corresponding character is set to
        -:220893:** 'x'. For those that remain as they are, the corresponding character of the
        -:220894:** rbu_control value should be set to '.'. For example, given the tables
        -:220895:** above, the update statement:
        -:220896:**
        -:220897:**   UPDATE t1 SET c = 'usa' WHERE a = 4;
        -:220898:**
        -:220899:** is represented by the data_t1 row created by:
        -:220900:**
        -:220901:**   INSERT INTO data_t1(a, b, c, rbu_control) VALUES(4, NULL, 'usa', '..x');
        -:220902:**
        -:220903:** Instead of an 'x' character, characters of the rbu_control value specified
        -:220904:** for UPDATEs may also be set to 'd'. In this case, instead of updating the
        -:220905:** target table with the value stored in the corresponding data_% column, the
        -:220906:** user-defined SQL function "rbu_delta()" is invoked and the result stored in
        -:220907:** the target table column. rbu_delta() is invoked with two arguments - the
        -:220908:** original value currently stored in the target table column and the
        -:220909:** value specified in the data_xxx table.
        -:220910:**
        -:220911:** For example, this row:
        -:220912:**
        -:220913:**   INSERT INTO data_t1(a, b, c, rbu_control) VALUES(4, NULL, 'usa', '..d');
        -:220914:**
        -:220915:** is similar to an UPDATE statement such as:
        -:220916:**
        -:220917:**   UPDATE t1 SET c = rbu_delta(c, 'usa') WHERE a = 4;
        -:220918:**
        -:220919:** Finally, if an 'f' character appears in place of a 'd' or 's' in an
        -:220920:** ota_control string, the contents of the data_xxx table column is assumed
        -:220921:** to be a "fossil delta" - a patch to be applied to a blob value in the
        -:220922:** format used by the fossil source-code management system. In this case
        -:220923:** the existing value within the target database table must be of type BLOB.
        -:220924:** It is replaced by the result of applying the specified fossil delta to
        -:220925:** itself.
        -:220926:**
        -:220927:** If the target database table is a virtual table or a table with no PRIMARY
        -:220928:** KEY, the rbu_control value should not include a character corresponding
        -:220929:** to the rbu_rowid value. For example, this:
        -:220930:**
        -:220931:**   INSERT INTO data_ft1(a, b, rbu_rowid, rbu_control)
        -:220932:**       VALUES(NULL, 'usa', 12, '.x');
        -:220933:**
        -:220934:** causes a result similar to:
        -:220935:**
        -:220936:**   UPDATE ft1 SET b = 'usa' WHERE rowid = 12;
        -:220937:**
        -:220938:** The data_xxx tables themselves should have no PRIMARY KEY declarations.
        -:220939:** However, RBU is more efficient if reading the rows in from each data_xxx
        -:220940:** table in "rowid" order is roughly the same as reading them sorted by
        -:220941:** the PRIMARY KEY of the corresponding target database table. In other
        -:220942:** words, rows should be sorted using the destination table PRIMARY KEY
        -:220943:** fields before they are inserted into the data_xxx tables.
        -:220944:**
        -:220945:** USAGE
        -:220946:**
        -:220947:** The API declared below allows an application to apply an RBU update
        -:220948:** stored on disk to an existing target database. Essentially, the
        -:220949:** application:
        -:220950:**
        -:220951:**     1) Opens an RBU handle using the sqlite3rbu_open() function.
        -:220952:**
        -:220953:**     2) Registers any required virtual table modules with the database
        -:220954:**        handle returned by sqlite3rbu_db(). Also, if required, register
        -:220955:**        the rbu_delta() implementation.
        -:220956:**
        -:220957:**     3) Calls the sqlite3rbu_step() function one or more times on
        -:220958:**        the new handle. Each call to sqlite3rbu_step() performs a single
        -:220959:**        b-tree operation, so thousands of calls may be required to apply
        -:220960:**        a complete update.
        -:220961:**
        -:220962:**     4) Calls sqlite3rbu_close() to close the RBU update handle. If
        -:220963:**        sqlite3rbu_step() has been called enough times to completely
        -:220964:**        apply the update to the target database, then the RBU database
        -:220965:**        is marked as fully applied. Otherwise, the state of the RBU
        -:220966:**        update application is saved in the RBU database for later
        -:220967:**        resumption.
        -:220968:**
        -:220969:** See comments below for more detail on APIs.
        -:220970:**
        -:220971:** If an update is only partially applied to the target database by the
        -:220972:** time sqlite3rbu_close() is called, various state information is saved
        -:220973:** within the RBU database. This allows subsequent processes to automatically
        -:220974:** resume the RBU update from where it left off.
        -:220975:**
        -:220976:** To remove all RBU extension state information, returning an RBU database
        -:220977:** to its original contents, it is sufficient to drop all tables that begin
        -:220978:** with the prefix "rbu_"
        -:220979:**
        -:220980:** DATABASE LOCKING
        -:220981:**
        -:220982:** An RBU update may not be applied to a database in WAL mode. Attempting
        -:220983:** to do so is an error (SQLITE_ERROR).
        -:220984:**
        -:220985:** While an RBU handle is open, a SHARED lock may be held on the target
        -:220986:** database file. This means it is possible for other clients to read the
        -:220987:** database, but not to write it.
        -:220988:**
        -:220989:** If an RBU update is started and then suspended before it is completed,
        -:220990:** then an external client writes to the database, then attempting to resume
        -:220991:** the suspended RBU update is also an error (SQLITE_BUSY).
        -:220992:*/
        -:220993:
        -:220994:#ifndef _SQLITE3RBU_H
        -:220995:#define _SQLITE3RBU_H
        -:220996:
        -:220997:/* #include "sqlite3.h"              ** Required for error code definitions ** */
        -:220998:
        -:220999:#if 0
        -:221000:extern "C" {
        -:221001:#endif
        -:221002:
        -:221003:typedef struct sqlite3rbu sqlite3rbu;
        -:221004:
        -:221005:/*
        -:221006:** Open an RBU handle.
        -:221007:**
        -:221008:** Argument zTarget is the path to the target database. Argument zRbu is
        -:221009:** the path to the RBU database. Each call to this function must be matched
        -:221010:** by a call to sqlite3rbu_close(). When opening the databases, RBU passes
        -:221011:** the SQLITE_CONFIG_URI flag to sqlite3_open_v2(). So if either zTarget
        -:221012:** or zRbu begin with "file:", it will be interpreted as an SQLite
        -:221013:** database URI, not a regular file name.
        -:221014:**
        -:221015:** If the zState argument is passed a NULL value, the RBU extension stores
        -:221016:** the current state of the update (how many rows have been updated, which
        -:221017:** indexes are yet to be updated etc.) within the RBU database itself. This
        -:221018:** can be convenient, as it means that the RBU application does not need to
        -:221019:** organize removing a separate state file after the update is concluded.
        -:221020:** Or, if zState is non-NULL, it must be a path to a database file in which
        -:221021:** the RBU extension can store the state of the update.
        -:221022:**
        -:221023:** When resuming an RBU update, the zState argument must be passed the same
        -:221024:** value as when the RBU update was started.
        -:221025:**
        -:221026:** Once the RBU update is finished, the RBU extension does not
        -:221027:** automatically remove any zState database file, even if it created it.
        -:221028:**
        -:221029:** By default, RBU uses the default VFS to access the files on disk. To
        -:221030:** use a VFS other than the default, an SQLite "file:" URI containing a
        -:221031:** "vfs=..." option may be passed as the zTarget option.
        -:221032:**
        -:221033:** IMPORTANT NOTE FOR ZIPVFS USERS: The RBU extension works with all of
        -:221034:** SQLite's built-in VFSs, including the multiplexor VFS. However it does
        -:221035:** not work out of the box with zipvfs. Refer to the comment describing
        -:221036:** the zipvfs_create_vfs() API below for details on using RBU with zipvfs.
        -:221037:*/
        -:221038:SQLITE_API sqlite3rbu *sqlite3rbu_open(
        -:221039:  const char *zTarget,
        -:221040:  const char *zRbu,
        -:221041:  const char *zState
        -:221042:);
        -:221043:
        -:221044:/*
        -:221045:** Open an RBU handle to perform an RBU vacuum on database file zTarget.
        -:221046:** An RBU vacuum is similar to SQLite's built-in VACUUM command, except
        -:221047:** that it can be suspended and resumed like an RBU update.
        -:221048:**
        -:221049:** The second argument to this function identifies a database in which
        -:221050:** to store the state of the RBU vacuum operation if it is suspended. The
        -:221051:** first time sqlite3rbu_vacuum() is called, to start an RBU vacuum
        -:221052:** operation, the state database should either not exist or be empty
        -:221053:** (contain no tables). If an RBU vacuum is suspended by calling
        -:221054:** sqlite3rbu_close() on the RBU handle before sqlite3rbu_step() has
        -:221055:** returned SQLITE_DONE, the vacuum state is stored in the state database.
        -:221056:** The vacuum can be resumed by calling this function to open a new RBU
        -:221057:** handle specifying the same target and state databases.
        -:221058:**
        -:221059:** If the second argument passed to this function is NULL, then the
        -:221060:** name of the state database is "<database>-vacuum", where <database>
        -:221061:** is the name of the target database file. In this case, on UNIX, if the
        -:221062:** state database is not already present in the file-system, it is created
        -:221063:** with the same permissions as the target db is made.
        -:221064:**
        -:221065:** With an RBU vacuum, it is an SQLITE_MISUSE error if the name of the
        -:221066:** state database ends with "-vactmp". This name is reserved for internal
        -:221067:** use.
        -:221068:**
        -:221069:** This function does not delete the state database after an RBU vacuum
        -:221070:** is completed, even if it created it. However, if the call to
        -:221071:** sqlite3rbu_close() returns any value other than SQLITE_OK, the contents
        -:221072:** of the state tables within the state database are zeroed. This way,
        -:221073:** the next call to sqlite3rbu_vacuum() opens a handle that starts a
        -:221074:** new RBU vacuum operation.
        -:221075:**
        -:221076:** As with sqlite3rbu_open(), Zipvfs users should refer to the comment
        -:221077:** describing the sqlite3rbu_create_vfs() API function below for
        -:221078:** a description of the complications associated with using RBU with
        -:221079:** zipvfs databases.
        -:221080:*/
        -:221081:SQLITE_API sqlite3rbu *sqlite3rbu_vacuum(
        -:221082:  const char *zTarget,
        -:221083:  const char *zState
        -:221084:);
        -:221085:
        -:221086:/*
        -:221087:** Configure a limit for the amount of temp space that may be used by
        -:221088:** the RBU handle passed as the first argument. The new limit is specified
        -:221089:** in bytes by the second parameter. If it is positive, the limit is updated.
        -:221090:** If the second parameter to this function is passed zero, then the limit
        -:221091:** is removed entirely. If the second parameter is negative, the limit is
        -:221092:** not modified (this is useful for querying the current limit).
        -:221093:**
        -:221094:** In all cases the returned value is the current limit in bytes (zero
        -:221095:** indicates unlimited).
        -:221096:**
        -:221097:** If the temp space limit is exceeded during operation, an SQLITE_FULL
        -:221098:** error is returned.
        -:221099:*/
        -:221100:SQLITE_API sqlite3_int64 sqlite3rbu_temp_size_limit(sqlite3rbu*, sqlite3_int64);
        -:221101:
        -:221102:/*
        -:221103:** Return the current amount of temp file space, in bytes, currently used by
        -:221104:** the RBU handle passed as the only argument.
        -:221105:*/
        -:221106:SQLITE_API sqlite3_int64 sqlite3rbu_temp_size(sqlite3rbu*);
        -:221107:
        -:221108:/*
        -:221109:** Internally, each RBU connection uses a separate SQLite database
        -:221110:** connection to access the target and rbu update databases. This
        -:221111:** API allows the application direct access to these database handles.
        -:221112:**
        -:221113:** The first argument passed to this function must be a valid, open, RBU
        -:221114:** handle. The second argument should be passed zero to access the target
        -:221115:** database handle, or non-zero to access the rbu update database handle.
        -:221116:** Accessing the underlying database handles may be useful in the
        -:221117:** following scenarios:
        -:221118:**
        -:221119:**   * If any target tables are virtual tables, it may be necessary to
        -:221120:**     call sqlite3_create_module() on the target database handle to
        -:221121:**     register the required virtual table implementations.
        -:221122:**
        -:221123:**   * If the data_xxx tables in the RBU source database are virtual
        -:221124:**     tables, the application may need to call sqlite3_create_module() on
        -:221125:**     the rbu update db handle to any required virtual table
        -:221126:**     implementations.
        -:221127:**
        -:221128:**   * If the application uses the "rbu_delta()" feature described above,
        -:221129:**     it must use sqlite3_create_function() or similar to register the
        -:221130:**     rbu_delta() implementation with the target database handle.
        -:221131:**
        -:221132:** If an error has occurred, either while opening or stepping the RBU object,
        -:221133:** this function may return NULL. The error code and message may be collected
        -:221134:** when sqlite3rbu_close() is called.
        -:221135:**
        -:221136:** Database handles returned by this function remain valid until the next
        -:221137:** call to any sqlite3rbu_xxx() function other than sqlite3rbu_db().
        -:221138:*/
        -:221139:SQLITE_API sqlite3 *sqlite3rbu_db(sqlite3rbu*, int bRbu);
        -:221140:
        -:221141:/*
        -:221142:** Do some work towards applying the RBU update to the target db.
        -:221143:**
        -:221144:** Return SQLITE_DONE if the update has been completely applied, or
        -:221145:** SQLITE_OK if no error occurs but there remains work to do to apply
        -:221146:** the RBU update. If an error does occur, some other error code is
        -:221147:** returned.
        -:221148:**
        -:221149:** Once a call to sqlite3rbu_step() has returned a value other than
        -:221150:** SQLITE_OK, all subsequent calls on the same RBU handle are no-ops
        -:221151:** that immediately return the same value.
        -:221152:*/
        -:221153:SQLITE_API int sqlite3rbu_step(sqlite3rbu *pRbu);
        -:221154:
        -:221155:/*
        -:221156:** Force RBU to save its state to disk.
        -:221157:**
        -:221158:** If a power failure or application crash occurs during an update, following
        -:221159:** system recovery RBU may resume the update from the point at which the state
        -:221160:** was last saved. In other words, from the most recent successful call to
        -:221161:** sqlite3rbu_close() or this function.
        -:221162:**
        -:221163:** SQLITE_OK is returned if successful, or an SQLite error code otherwise.
        -:221164:*/
        -:221165:SQLITE_API int sqlite3rbu_savestate(sqlite3rbu *pRbu);
        -:221166:
        -:221167:/*
        -:221168:** Close an RBU handle.
        -:221169:**
        -:221170:** If the RBU update has been completely applied, mark the RBU database
        -:221171:** as fully applied. Otherwise, assuming no error has occurred, save the
        -:221172:** current state of the RBU update application to the RBU database.
        -:221173:**
        -:221174:** If an error has already occurred as part of an sqlite3rbu_step()
        -:221175:** or sqlite3rbu_open() call, or if one occurs within this function, an
        -:221176:** SQLite error code is returned. Additionally, if pzErrmsg is not NULL,
        -:221177:** *pzErrmsg may be set to point to a buffer containing a utf-8 formatted
        -:221178:** English language error message. It is the responsibility of the caller to
        -:221179:** eventually free any such buffer using sqlite3_free().
        -:221180:**
        -:221181:** Otherwise, if no error occurs, this function returns SQLITE_OK if the
        -:221182:** update has been partially applied, or SQLITE_DONE if it has been
        -:221183:** completely applied.
        -:221184:*/
        -:221185:SQLITE_API int sqlite3rbu_close(sqlite3rbu *pRbu, char **pzErrmsg);
        -:221186:
        -:221187:/*
        -:221188:** Return the total number of key-value operations (inserts, deletes or
        -:221189:** updates) that have been performed on the target database since the
        -:221190:** current RBU update was started.
        -:221191:*/
        -:221192:SQLITE_API sqlite3_int64 sqlite3rbu_progress(sqlite3rbu *pRbu);
        -:221193:
        -:221194:/*
        -:221195:** Obtain permyriadage (permyriadage is to 10000 as percentage is to 100)
        -:221196:** progress indications for the two stages of an RBU update. This API may
        -:221197:** be useful for driving GUI progress indicators and similar.
        -:221198:**
        -:221199:** An RBU update is divided into two stages:
        -:221200:**
        -:221201:**   * Stage 1, in which changes are accumulated in an oal/wal file, and
        -:221202:**   * Stage 2, in which the contents of the wal file are copied into the
        -:221203:**     main database.
        -:221204:**
        -:221205:** The update is visible to non-RBU clients during stage 2. During stage 1
        -:221206:** non-RBU reader clients may see the original database.
        -:221207:**
        -:221208:** If this API is called during stage 2 of the update, output variable
        -:221209:** (*pnOne) is set to 10000 to indicate that stage 1 has finished and (*pnTwo)
        -:221210:** to a value between 0 and 10000 to indicate the permyriadage progress of
        -:221211:** stage 2. A value of 5000 indicates that stage 2 is half finished,
        -:221212:** 9000 indicates that it is 90% finished, and so on.
        -:221213:**
        -:221214:** If this API is called during stage 1 of the update, output variable
        -:221215:** (*pnTwo) is set to 0 to indicate that stage 2 has not yet started. The
        -:221216:** value to which (*pnOne) is set depends on whether or not the RBU
        -:221217:** database contains an "rbu_count" table. The rbu_count table, if it
        -:221218:** exists, must contain the same columns as the following:
        -:221219:**
        -:221220:**   CREATE TABLE rbu_count(tbl TEXT PRIMARY KEY, cnt INTEGER) WITHOUT ROWID;
        -:221221:**
        -:221222:** There must be one row in the table for each source (data_xxx) table within
        -:221223:** the RBU database. The 'tbl' column should contain the name of the source
        -:221224:** table. The 'cnt' column should contain the number of rows within the
        -:221225:** source table.
        -:221226:**
        -:221227:** If the rbu_count table is present and populated correctly and this
        -:221228:** API is called during stage 1, the *pnOne output variable is set to the
        -:221229:** permyriadage progress of the same stage. If the rbu_count table does
        -:221230:** not exist, then (*pnOne) is set to -1 during stage 1. If the rbu_count
        -:221231:** table exists but is not correctly populated, the value of the *pnOne
        -:221232:** output variable during stage 1 is undefined.
        -:221233:*/
        -:221234:SQLITE_API void sqlite3rbu_bp_progress(sqlite3rbu *pRbu, int *pnOne, int*pnTwo);
