        -:221612:#ifndef UNUSED_PARAMETER
        -:221613:/*
        -:221614:** The following macros are used to suppress compiler warnings and to
        -:221615:** make it clear to human readers when a function parameter is deliberately
        -:221616:** left unused within the body of a function. This usually happens when
        -:221617:** a function is called via a function pointer. For example the
        -:221618:** implementation of an SQL aggregate step callback may not use the
        -:221619:** parameter indicating the number of arguments passed to the aggregate,
        -:221620:** if it knows that this is enforced elsewhere.
        -:221621:**
        -:221622:** When a function parameter is not used at all within the body of a function,
        -:221623:** it is generally named "NotUsed" or "NotUsed2" to make things even clearer.
        -:221624:** However, these macros may also be used to suppress warnings related to
        -:221625:** parameters that may or may not be used depending on compilation options.
        -:221626:** For example those parameters only used in assert() statements. In these
        -:221627:** cases the parameters are named as per the usual conventions.
        -:221628:*/
        -:221629:#define UNUSED_PARAMETER(x) (void)(x)
