        -:228290:
        -:228291:/*
        -:228292:** RECORD FORMAT:
        -:228293:**
        -:228294:** The following record format is similar to (but not compatible with) that
        -:228295:** used in SQLite database files. This format is used as part of the
        -:228296:** change-set binary format, and so must be architecture independent.
        -:228297:**
        -:228298:** Unlike the SQLite database record format, each field is self-contained -
        -:228299:** there is no separation of header and data. Each field begins with a
        -:228300:** single byte describing its type, as follows:
        -:228301:**
        -:228302:**       0x00: Undefined value.
        -:228303:**       0x01: Integer value.
        -:228304:**       0x02: Real value.
        -:228305:**       0x03: Text value.
        -:228306:**       0x04: Blob value.
        -:228307:**       0x05: SQL NULL value.
        -:228308:**
        -:228309:** Note that the above match the definitions of SQLITE_INTEGER, SQLITE_TEXT
        -:228310:** and so on in sqlite3.h. For undefined and NULL values, the field consists
        -:228311:** only of the single type byte. For other types of values, the type byte
        -:228312:** is followed by:
        -:228313:**
        -:228314:**   Text values:
        -:228315:**     A varint containing the number of bytes in the value (encoded using
        -:228316:**     UTF-8). Followed by a buffer containing the UTF-8 representation
        -:228317:**     of the text value. There is no nul terminator.
        -:228318:**
        -:228319:**   Blob values:
        -:228320:**     A varint containing the number of bytes in the value, followed by
        -:228321:**     a buffer containing the value itself.
        -:228322:**
        -:228323:**   Integer values:
        -:228324:**     An 8-byte big-endian integer value.
        -:228325:**
        -:228326:**   Real values:
        -:228327:**     An 8-byte big-endian IEEE 754-2008 real value.
        -:228328:**
        -:228329:** Varint values are encoded in the same way as varints in the SQLite
        -:228330:** record format.
        -:228331:**
        -:228332:** CHANGESET FORMAT:
        -:228333:**
        -:228334:** A changeset is a collection of DELETE, UPDATE and INSERT operations on
        -:228335:** one or more tables. Operations on a single table are grouped together,
        -:228336:** but may occur in any order (i.e. deletes, updates and inserts are all
        -:228337:** mixed together).
        -:228338:**
        -:228339:** Each group of changes begins with a table header:
        -:228340:**
        -:228341:**   1 byte: Constant 0x54 (capital 'T')
        -:228342:**   Varint: Number of columns in the table.
        -:228343:**   nCol bytes: 0x01 for PK columns, 0x00 otherwise.
        -:228344:**   N bytes: Unqualified table name (encoded using UTF-8). Nul-terminated.
        -:228345:**
        -:228346:** Followed by one or more changes to the table.
        -:228347:**
        -:228348:**   1 byte: Either SQLITE_INSERT (0x12), UPDATE (0x17) or DELETE (0x09).
        -:228349:**   1 byte: The "indirect-change" flag.
        -:228350:**   old.* record: (delete and update only)
        -:228351:**   new.* record: (insert and update only)
        -:228352:**
        -:228353:** The "old.*" and "new.*" records, if present, are N field records in the
        -:228354:** format described above under "RECORD FORMAT", where N is the number of
        -:228355:** columns in the table. The i'th field of each record is associated with
        -:228356:** the i'th column of the table, counting from left to right in the order
        -:228357:** in which columns were declared in the CREATE TABLE statement.
        -:228358:**
        -:228359:** The new.* record that is part of each INSERT change contains the values
        -:228360:** that make up the new row. Similarly, the old.* record that is part of each
        -:228361:** DELETE change contains the values that made up the row that was deleted
        -:228362:** from the database. In the changeset format, the records that are part
        -:228363:** of INSERT or DELETE changes never contain any undefined (type byte 0x00)
        -:228364:** fields.
        -:228365:**
        -:228366:** Within the old.* record associated with an UPDATE change, all fields
        -:228367:** associated with table columns that are not PRIMARY KEY columns and are
        -:228368:** not modified by the UPDATE change are set to "undefined". Other fields
        -:228369:** are set to the values that made up the row before the UPDATE that the
        -:228370:** change records took place. Within the new.* record, fields associated
        -:228371:** with table columns modified by the UPDATE change contain the new
        -:228372:** values. Fields associated with table columns that are not modified
        -:228373:** are set to "undefined".
        -:228374:**
        -:228375:** PATCHSET FORMAT:
        -:228376:**
        -:228377:** A patchset is also a collection of changes. It is similar to a changeset,
        -:228378:** but leaves undefined those fields that are not useful if no conflict
        -:228379:** resolution is required when applying the changeset.
        -:228380:**
        -:228381:** Each group of changes begins with a table header:
        -:228382:**
        -:228383:**   1 byte: Constant 0x50 (capital 'P')
        -:228384:**   Varint: Number of columns in the table.
        -:228385:**   nCol bytes: 0x01 for PK columns, 0x00 otherwise.
        -:228386:**   N bytes: Unqualified table name (encoded using UTF-8). Nul-terminated.
        -:228387:**
        -:228388:** Followed by one or more changes to the table.
        -:228389:**
        -:228390:**   1 byte: Either SQLITE_INSERT (0x12), UPDATE (0x17) or DELETE (0x09).
        -:228391:**   1 byte: The "indirect-change" flag.
        -:228392:**   single record: (PK fields for DELETE, PK and modified fields for UPDATE,
        -:228393:**                   full record for INSERT).
        -:228394:**
        -:228395:** As in the changeset format, each field of the single record that is part
        -:228396:** of a patchset change is associated with the correspondingly positioned
        -:228397:** table column, counting from left to right within the CREATE TABLE
        -:228398:** statement.
        -:228399:**
        -:228400:** For a DELETE change, all fields within the record except those associated
        -:228401:** with PRIMARY KEY columns are omitted. The PRIMARY KEY fields contain the
        -:228402:** values identifying the row to delete.
        -:228403:**
        -:228404:** For an UPDATE change, all fields except those associated with PRIMARY KEY
        -:228405:** columns and columns that are modified by the UPDATE are set to "undefined".
        -:228406:** PRIMARY KEY fields contain the values identifying the table row to update,
        -:228407:** and fields associated with modified columns contain the new column values.
        -:228408:**
        -:228409:** The records associated with INSERT changes are in the same format as for
        -:228410:** changesets. It is not possible for a record associated with an INSERT
        -:228411:** change to contain a field set to "undefined".
        -:228412:**
        -:228413:** REBASE BLOB FORMAT:
        -:228414:**
        -:228415:** A rebase blob may be output by sqlite3changeset_apply_v2() and its
        -:228416:** streaming equivalent for use with the sqlite3_rebaser APIs to rebase
        -:228417:** existing changesets. A rebase blob contains one entry for each conflict
        -:228418:** resolved using either the OMIT or REPLACE strategies within the apply_v2()
        -:228419:** call.
        -:228420:**
        -:228421:** The format used for a rebase blob is very similar to that used for
        -:228422:** changesets. All entries related to a single table are grouped together.
        -:228423:**
        -:228424:** Each group of entries begins with a table header in changeset format:
        -:228425:**
        -:228426:**   1 byte: Constant 0x54 (capital 'T')
        -:228427:**   Varint: Number of columns in the table.
        -:228428:**   nCol bytes: 0x01 for PK columns, 0x00 otherwise.
        -:228429:**   N bytes: Unqualified table name (encoded using UTF-8). Nul-terminated.
        -:228430:**
        -:228431:** Followed by one or more entries associated with the table.
        -:228432:**
        -:228433:**   1 byte: Either SQLITE_INSERT (0x12), DELETE (0x09).
        -:228434:**   1 byte: Flag. 0x01 for REPLACE, 0x00 for OMIT.
        -:228435:**   record: (in the record format defined above).
        -:228436:**
        -:228437:** In a rebase blob, the first field is set to SQLITE_INSERT if the change
        -:228438:** that caused the conflict was an INSERT or UPDATE, or to SQLITE_DELETE if
        -:228439:** it was a DELETE. The second field is set to 0x01 if the conflict
        -:228440:** resolution strategy was REPLACE, or 0x00 if it was OMIT.
        -:228441:**
        -:228442:** If the change that caused the conflict was a DELETE, then the single
        -:228443:** record is a copy of the old.* record from the original changeset. If it
        -:228444:** was an INSERT, then the single record is a copy of the new.* record. If
        -:228445:** the conflicting change was an UPDATE, then the single record is a copy
        -:228446:** of the new.* record with the PK fields filled in based on the original
        -:228447:** old.* record.
        -:228448:*/
        -:228449:
        -:228450:/*
        -:228451:** For each row modified during a session, there exists a single instance of
        -:228452:** this structure stored in a SessionTable.aChange[] hash table.
        -:228453:*/
        -:228454:struct SessionChange {
        -:228455:  u8 op;                          /* One of UPDATE, DELETE, INSERT */
        -:228456:  u8 bIndirect;                   /* True if this change is "indirect" */
        -:228457:  u16 nRecordField;               /* Number of fields in aRecord[] */
        -:228458:  int nMaxSize;                   /* Max size of eventual changeset record */
        -:228459:  int nRecord;                    /* Number of bytes in buffer aRecord[] */
        -:228460:  u8 *aRecord;                    /* Buffer containing old.* record */
        -:228461:  SessionChange *pNext;           /* For hash-table collisions */
        -:228462:};
        -:228463:
        -:228464:/*
        -:228465:** Write a varint with value iVal into the buffer at aBuf. Return the
